Every content site eventually hits the same failure mode. The blog started as a folder of markdown files, frontmatter hand-typed, and it worked — until a post shipped with `publishDate` instead of `date`, a draft got deployed because nothing filtered it, and the listing page turned into a pile of sort functions nobody trusts. The problem was never markdown; it was that the content had no *contract*. Astro's answer is content collections: every markdown and MDX file in a collection gets a zod schema validated at build time, TypeScript types inferred from that schema, and a query API to read it all. This is the load-bearing chapter of the book — blogs, docs, changelogs, even this book's own chapters are collections — and we will build one running example throughout: a blog with authors.

## The problem with loose markdown

A folder of markdown files is a database with no schema, and the bugs are the kind that only appear in production. Typos in frontmatter keys pass silently and surface as blank titles. Dates arrive as strings in three formats, and sorting them in page code means everyone re-implements the parse. Drafts need a filter written correctly in every page that lists posts. And nothing tells you a post is missing a required field until a visitor sees `undefined` where a byline should be.

Collections fix this by moving the contract out of page code and into one place, then enforcing it when you build. A file that violates the schema fails the build with the offending file and field named — the same fail-early philosophy as `getStaticPaths` in Chapter 2. The page code stops defending itself, because the type system guarantees what an entry looks like.

## Defining a collection

A collection is declared in `src/content.config.ts` and backed by a *loader* — the code that reads entries from somewhere and hands Astro their ids and data. For files on disk, the glob loader is the standard choice; other loaders can pull from a CMS or an API, which we touch on in Chapter 6. Here is the running example, a blog plus the authors who write it:

```ts
// src/content.config.ts
import { defineCollection, z } from "astro:content";
import { glob } from "astro/loaders";

const blog = defineCollection({
  loader: glob({ pattern: "**/*.mdx", base: "./src/content/blog" }),
  schema: z.object({
    title: z.string(),
    description: z.string(),
    pubDate: z.coerce.date(),
    draft: z.boolean().default(false),
    author: z.string(),
  }),
});

const authors = defineCollection({
  loader: glob({ pattern: "**/*.yaml", base: "./src/content/authors" }),
  schema: z.object({
    name: z.string(),
    role: z.string(),
  }),
});

export const collections = { blog, authors };
```

The glob loader watches `src/content/blog` recursively, and each file's path becomes its id: `src/content/blog/2025/hello-astro.mdx` is queryable as `2025/hello-astro`. The moment this config exists, `astro sync` generates types for every entry, and anything that queries the collection gets them. Note that the schema lives *with* the loader — one declaration owns where entries come from and what shape they are, and no page in the project has to know either.

## Schemas with zod

The schema is where the contract gets specific, and zod's vocabulary maps cleanly onto content concerns. Coerce messy input into real types, restrict fields to closed sets, mark optional fields, and give defaults for the common case:

```ts
const blog = defineCollection({
  loader: glob({ pattern: "**/*.mdx", base: "./src/content/blog" }),
  schema: z.object({
    title: z.string(),
    description: z.string().max(160),
    pubDate: z.coerce.date(),
    tags: z.array(z.enum(["frontend", "tooling", "performance"])).default([]),
    draft: z.boolean().default(false),
    author: z.string(),
  }),
});
```

With this in place, `"2025-01-14"` becomes a real `Date` at load time, a `tags: ["weird"]` is a build error naming the file, and every page that reads `post.data.tags` knows the array exists and contains only those three strings — no filtering, no fallbacks. The build-time failure is the feature working: it is exactly the typo-driven bug that loose markdown shipped silently, caught on your machine instead of in a browser. Schemas are also where editorial policy becomes enforceable; if the style guide says descriptions cap at 160 characters, that is now a `.max(160)` instead of a memo.

## Querying with getCollection and getEntry

Two functions cover almost every read. `getCollection("blog")` returns every entry in the collection; `getEntry("blog", id)` fetches one. Both return entries whose `data` is fully typed from the schema, and both accept a filter callback so the query layer stays in one place:

```astro
---
// src/pages/blog/index.astro
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import PostCard from "../../components/PostCard.astro";

const posts = await getCollection(
  "blog",
  ({ data }) => !data.draft,
).then((entries) =>
  entries.sort((a, b) => b.data.pubDate.valueOf() - a.data.pubDate.valueOf()),
);
---
<ul>
  {posts.map((post) => <PostCard post={post} />)}
</ul>
```

The draft filter lives here once, not in every listing; the sort is over real `Date` objects because the schema coerced them. `getEntry` is the single-post counterpart — by id, with the same types — and it is also how you resolve a cross-collection lookup, which the next section makes common. One habit to carry over from Chapter 2: do this querying in `getStaticPaths` when a route needs it, so the census does the work once and the page receives props.

## Rendering entries

An entry's markdown body is not a string you render yourself — Astro compiles it, and the `render()` function from `astro:content` hands you the finished component:

```astro
---
// src/pages/blog/[slug].astro
import { getCollection, getEntry, render } from "astro:content";
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const posts = await getCollection("blog", ({ data }) => !data.draft);
  return posts.map((post) => ({ params: { slug: post.id }, props: { post } }));
}

const { post } = Astro.props;
const { Content, headings } = await render(post);
const author = await getEntry("authors", post.data.author);
---
<BaseLayout title={post.data.title} description={post.data.description}>
  <p>By {author.data.name}</p>
  <article><Content /></article>
</BaseLayout>
```

`render()` also returns `headings` — the outline of the document, ready to build a table of contents — and remark plugin metadata. The important part is what is missing: no markdown-it configuration, no sanitizing, no bespoke parsing in your code. The dynamic route here is exactly the Chapter 2 pattern, now fed by a collection instead of ad-hoc file reads, and the type-checked pipeline runs unbroken from schema to rendered HTML.

## References between collections

The example above reached into `authors` by hand, and that hand-rolled lookup is the first thing to rot when ids get renamed. `reference()` makes the relation explicit — the schema declares that `author` points at the authors collection, and Astro's types carry that fact everywhere:

```ts
import { reference, z } from "astro:content";

const blog = defineCollection({
  loader: glob({ pattern: "**/*.mdx", base: "./src/content/blog" }),
  schema: z.object({
    title: z.string(),
    pubDate: z.coerce.date(),
    draft: z.boolean().default(false),
    author: reference("authors"),
  }),
});
```

In frontmatter, the author is still written as a plain id — `author: "rin"` — but when read back, `post.data.author` is a reference object you can resolve in one call:

```astro
---
const author = await getEntry(post.data.author);
---
<AuthorChip name={author.data.name} role={author.data.role} />
```

If an id stops matching — an author file renamed, a typo in frontmatter — the build fails at validation instead of shipping a byline of `undefined`. The same mechanism handles any relation your content has: posts to series, docs to products, changelog entries to releases. Model relations in the schema and the query code stays boring, which is the highest compliment content code can receive.

## MDX inside collections

MDX is markdown that imports, and collections treat it as a first-class citizen: point the glob loader's pattern at `*.mdx`, and entries can use components directly in the body, with the same schema and query API as plain markdown:

```mdx
---
title: "Static is a feature"
description: "Rendering at build time is not a limitation."
pubDate: 2025-01-14
author: rin
---

import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";

Renders happen once, on the build machine.

<Callout kind="tip">Every dynamic route still needs `getStaticPaths`.</Callout>
```

The imports resolve through Vite like any other module, the components are the typed, slotted components of Chapter 3, and the schema validates the frontmatter identically. One practical note: because a collection can hold both extensions, a single glob with `**/*.{md,mdx}` keeps authors free to choose per post. There is one caveat worth respecting — a component imported into MDX renders for every entry that imports it, so keep the expensive ones out of content the way you would keep them out of a hot path anywhere else.

## Key takeaways

- Collections give markdown and MDX a contract: a glob loader for where entries come from and a zod schema for what they must contain, both declared in `src/content.config.ts`.
- Schema violations fail the build with the file and field named, which converts silent production bugs into loud local ones.
- `getCollection` and `getEntry` return fully typed entries; filtering and sorting belong at the query site, and `getStaticPaths` should absorb the work once per route.
- `render(entry)` compiles an entry's body into a `Content` component, with `headings` for outlines — no manual markdown parsing anywhere.
- `reference()` declares relations between collections, and `getEntry(post.data.author)` resolves them with the same build-time safety as everything else.

Content now has a spine — but the pages built on it are still static. The moment a component needs to actually *run* in the browser, you need islands, and that is where the book goes next.

So far every page in this book has been built ahead of time, and that is the default — but not a law. The real question underneath every rendering framework is: *who computes this HTML, and when?* For Astro the answer is per page: this one at build time, that one on every request, and — since Astro 5 — even a static page with one server-rendered island bolted into it. This chapter covers the dial and its detents: build-time fetching, the `prerender` flag, adapters, API endpoints, server islands, and the environment variables that keep secrets on the machine that needs them.

## Where does a page come from?

Three honest answers exist, and each has a cost profile. *Build time*: the HTML is computed once when you deploy, cached everywhere, served from a CDN, and stale until the next build. *Request time*: the HTML is computed on every request — always current, slower, and running on a server you pay to keep warm. *Hybrid*: the page is static and one piece of it is computed per request. Most frameworks pick one answer globally; Astro picks two defaults and a dial. The default is build time — the zero-JS, cache-forever pages of Chapters 1 through 5 — with `export const prerender = false` as the per-page escape hatch, and server islands as the fine-grained hybrid. Choosing per page is the point: a changelog and a stock ticker have no business obeying the same policy.

## Fetching at build time

Top-level `await` in the frontmatter is the build-time fetch, and you have used it since Chapter 1 without ceremony. The mechanics are worth one honest look, because the mental model differs from client-side fetching: the call runs once, during `astro build`, on the machine running the build.

```astro
---
// src/pages/team.astro
const res = await fetch("https://api.example.com/v2/team", {
  headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${Astro.env.CMS_TOKEN}` },
});
const team = await res.json();
---
<ul>
  {team.map((member) => (
    <li>{member.name} — {member.role}</li>
  ))}
</ul>
```

The token in that header is safe by construction — nothing in the frontmatter ever reaches a browser, a point this chapter returns to. The trade is staleness: the page shows the team as of the last deploy. For content that changes when you ship — posts, docs, pricing tiers — that is not a limitation but the correct cache policy. The operational habit is to make the build the update trigger: most CMSs offer a deploy webhook, so "content changed" becomes "rebuild," and the fetch's once-per-build cost amortizes over every request until the next one. If a page's data changes *without* a deploy, that is the signal it belongs in the next section.

## prerender as a dial

The escape hatch is one line. In Astro 5 there are two output modes: `static` (the default), where every page is prerendered unless it opts out, and `server`, where the default flips. The flag lives in the page:

```astro
---
// src/pages/rates.astro
export const prerender = false;

const res = await fetch("https://api.example.com/v2/rates");
const rates = await res.json();
---
<p>EUR/USD as of {new Date().toISOString()}: {rates.eurUsd}</p>
```

That page is now computed per request, forever current, and it needs an *adapter* to exist at all — a package that teaches Astro how to run on a given platform:

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from "astro/config";
import node from "@astrojs/node";

export default defineConfig({
  output: "static",
  adapter: node({ mode: "standalone" }),
});
```

The shape of this is worth savoring: `output: "static"` with an adapter installed means every page is a file *except* the ones that ask otherwise — the hybrid behavior older frameworks needed a special mode for. `prerender = true` on a server-output project works the same way in reverse. The adapter choice follows the host — Node for your own container, Vercel or Netlify or Cloudflare for their platforms — and it changes nothing about the page code, which is the point. A route that cannot be known at build time (Chapter 2's caveat) and data that changes without a deploy are the two honest reasons to flip the flag; anything else is usually a caching problem wearing a rendering-problem costume.

## API endpoints

Pages render HTML; endpoints return data, and in Astro they are the same kind of file. Export functions named after HTTP methods from any file in `src/pages`:

```ts
// src/pages/api/search.ts
import type { APIRoute } from "astro";
import { searchPosts } from "../../lib/search";

export const prerender = false;

export const GET: APIRoute = async ({ url }) => {
  const query = url.searchParams.get("q") ?? "";
  return Response.json({ results: await searchPosts(query) });
};

export const POST: APIRoute = async ({ request }) => {
  const body = await request.json();
  // ...handle submission
  return Response.json({ received: true });
};
```

Naming the file `search.json.ts` instead serves `/api/search.json`, letting the extension carry the content type. One subtlety ties back to the dial: a `GET` endpoint *without* `prerender = false` is prerendered like a page — which is exactly right for a build-time search index snapshot, and exactly wrong for a live lookup. Non-`GET` methods require request-time rendering, full stop. Endpoints are also the escape hatch for things islands shouldn't do inline: an island that needs the search index should call an endpoint rather than ship the index as props, a boundary Chapter 5 drew for exactly this reason. For form handling specifically, though, there is a better tool than raw endpoints — actions — and they get their own stage in Chapter 8.

## Server islands

The hybrid deserves its own section, because Astro 5 made it a first-class mechanism. A *server island* is a server-rendered component embedded in an otherwise static page: the page ships from cache, and the island's HTML is computed per request and swapped in by a small script. Mark a component with `server:defer`, and mark the component itself as non-prerendered:

```astro
---
// src/components/StockTicker.astro
export const prerender = false;

const quotes = await getQuotes();
---
<dl>
  {quotes.map((q) => (
    <div><dt>{q.symbol}</dt><dd>{q.price}</dd></div>
  ))}
</dl>
```

```astro
---
// src/pages/index.astro — a fully static page
import StockTicker from "../components/StockTicker.astro";
---
<html lang="en">
  <body>
    <h1>Markets, mostly static</h1>
    <StockTicker server:defer>
      <p slot="fallback">Loading quotes…</p>
    </StockTicker>
  </body>
</html>
```

The page's HTML — the entire marketing site around the ticker — is prerendered, cached, and served from the edge. The ticker is not: Astro injects a request for it after load, the named `fallback` slot renders meanwhile, and the finished HTML replaces it. The cache hit ratio of a static page and the freshness of a dynamic one, per component. Note what this replaces: the old options were "make the whole page dynamic to personalize one widget" or "ship the widget's data to the client and render it there." Server islands are also streamed sensibly — the response shell goes out immediately and slow per-request parts flush as they resolve, so the fallback pattern doubles as a loading state rather than a lie.

## Environment variables and secrets

Rendering modes decide where code runs, and where code runs decides which variables it may see. Astro follows the Vite convention with one rule that matters: variables prefixed `PUBLIC_` are exposed to client-side code; everything else is private, available only in server-side code — frontmatter, endpoints, actions, middleware — and never bundled for the browser:

```bash
# .env
PUBLIC_SEARCH_URL=https://search.example.com
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_abc123
```

```astro
---
// src/pages/checkout.astro — server-side only
const key = Astro.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY;              // private: stays on the server
const searchUrl = import.meta.env.PUBLIC_SEARCH_URL;   // public: usable anywhere
---
<SearchWidget client:visible endpoint={searchUrl} />
```

`Astro.env` is the server-side accessor — readable in frontmatter and endpoints without ceremony — while `import.meta.env` works anywhere and is the only way to reach a `PUBLIC_` variable from a client script or an island. The rule to internalize: assume anything reachable from island code or a client script is public, because the browser received it. Tokens for the CMS in the build-time fetch, keys for the payment API in the request-time page — all safe in `Astro.env` because that code runs on the machine you control. CI is the same story: set secrets in the pipeline, not in committed files, and Chapter 9 wires that up for real.

## Key takeaways

- Every page answers "who computes this HTML, and when?" — build time by default, per request with `prerender = false`, or per component with a server island.
- Build-time fetching runs once per deploy on the build machine, making staleness a cache policy rather than a bug; wire deploys to content changes.
- Astro 5 has two output modes, `static` and `server`, and an adapter enables per-request rendering; the adapter choice follows the host and leaves page code untouched.
- Endpoints are pages that return data — `GET` can be prerendered, other methods cannot, and islands should call endpoints rather than carry payloads.
- Server islands give a static page a dynamic component: `server:defer` plus a fallback slot buys per-request freshness at per-component cost.
- `PUBLIC_`-prefixed variables reach the browser; everything else in `Astro.env` is server-only, and anything an island can read should be assumed public.

The rendering dial is set, the data sources are wired. What is left is the part visitors actually see first — the styling and the images — and Astro has opinions there too.

Styling is where component models usually leak. Global CSS collides, frameworks ship runtime style solutions, and images break layouts in ways that only show on your colleague's phone. Astro's approach is deliberately boring: styles in a component are scoped to that component at compile time, global styles are global because you said so, and images go through a build step that resizes, reformats, and refuses to ship a layout-breaking photo. Boring here is a compliment — none of this needs a runtime, which keeps the zero-JS budget from Chapter 1 intact.

## Scoped styles by default

A `<style>` tag in an `.astro` file is scoped: its selectors apply only to the markup in that same file. The mechanism is worth seeing, because it explains every surprise later. Astro rewrites both sides at compile time — each element in the template gets a generated attribute, and each selector gains a match on it:

```astro
---
// src/components/Note.astro
interface Props {
  tone?: "info" | "warn";
}
const { tone = "info" } = Astro.props;
---
<p class="note">Heads up: builds fail loudly now.</p>
<style>
  .note {
    border-left: 3px solid steelblue;
    padding-left: 0.75rem;
  }
</style>
```

```html
<p class="note" data-astro-cid-3jf2acqk>Heads up: builds fail loudly now.</p>
<style>
  .note[data-astro-cid-3jf2acqk] {
    border-left: 3px solid steelblue;
    padding-left: 0.75rem;
  }
</style>
```

Nothing is renamed — your class names survive to the browser intact, so devtools stay readable — but the selector cannot match anything outside the component, because only this component's elements carry that `cid`. Two components can both define `.note` on the same page and neither sees the other. The scoping is a *feature of the file*, not of where the style ends up: Astro hoists all component styles into the page's CSS at build, and the attribute is what keeps the hoisted rules from bleeding into each other.

## Escaping scope with :global and is:global

Scoped styles have a blind spot by design: they cannot reach markup they don't own. A scoped `.card h2` will not style a heading rendered by a child component or an island — each file's styles are scoped to that file, so the parent's selectors never see the child's DOM. When you genuinely need to reach across, say so explicitly:

```astro
---
// src/pages/blog/[slug].astro
---
<article class="prose">
  <slot />   <!-- markdown-rendered HTML, no component of yours owns it -->
</article>
<style>
  /* every h2 inside .prose, however deep it came from */
  .prose :global(h2) {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    font-size: 1.5rem;
  }
</style>

<style is:global>
  .prose pre {
    background: #1a1a2e;
    border-radius: 6px;
  }
</style>
```

The `:global(...)` wrapper leaves that one selector unscoped, anchored to your scoped `.prose` so it still cannot escape the component. For a whole stylesheet that should be global — a markdown theme, a print sheet — `is:global` on the style tag opts the entire block out.

The asymmetry answers a question that confuses everyone at first: *why don't my styles leak into imported components?* Because scoping is per file — the parent's rules get a `cid` the child's elements don't carry. That is the correct behavior most of the time; `:global` is how you override it locally, and a global reset sheet in `src/styles` is how you handle the cases that were never component work to begin with.

## Cascading order and specificity surprises

Scoping does not repeal the cascade, and two details bite everyone once. First, *order*: component styles are bundled into the page in the order components render, and when two rules of equal specificity target the same element, the later one wins — so a page-level `<style>` beats a component's rule for an element the page itself owns, and import order silently arbitrates everything else. Second, *specificity*: the scoping attribute is an attribute selector, which adds a class-level bump, so a scoped `.note` is slightly more specific than a global `.note` — usually what you want, occasionally baffling when a global utility loses to a scoped rule it "should" beat.

```css
/* Equal intent, unequal fate:
   global utility           .text-lg { font-size: 1.125rem; }        (0,1,0)
   scoped component rule    .meta[data-astro-cid-x] { ... }          (0,2,0)
   The scoped rule wins on specificity, regardless of order. */
```

The habits that keep this tractable: keep selectors flat — one class, no descendant chains — so specificity stays uniform and order stops mattering; put overrides where the element lives rather than reaching in from a parent; and treat `!important` as a review comment, not a fix. If a styling conflict feels like a fight, the real problem is usually that two components are styling the same element, and the fix is a slot or a prop, not a heavier selector.

## Tailwind, when you want it

Astro has no styling religion, and Tailwind slots in cleanly when your fingers already speak it. With Tailwind 4, the setup is the Vite plugin — no `tailwind.config.js`, no PostCSS wiring:

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from "astro/config";
import tailwindcss from "@tailwindcss/vite";

export default defineConfig({
  vite: {
    plugins: [tailwindcss()],
  },
});
```

```css
/* src/styles/global.css */
@import "tailwindcss";
```

Import that stylesheet once in `BaseLayout` and utility classes work in every component. You will still meet the older `@astrojs/tailwind` integration in tutorials — it is the Tailwind 3 path, and on Tailwind 4 the Vite plugin is the current route. The scoped-style machinery from the first section composes rather than conflicts: utilities in markup, a small `<style>` block for the few genuinely component-specific rules, and `:global()` for the markdown content Tailwind cannot reach with plain classes. The honest trade to weigh: utilities keep decisions co-located and delete dead CSS automatically, while scoped plain CSS keeps components portable — and neither should you push the decision until a choice starts hurting.

## Images with astro:assets

Images are the heaviest thing most pages ship, and `astro:assets` turns them into a build concern. Put source images in `src/assets`, and the `Image` component resizes and reformats at build time:

```astro
---
// src/pages/index.astro
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import hero from "../assets/harbor.jpg";
---
<Image
  src={hero}
  alt="Harbor at dawn"
  width={1200}
  height={630}
  format="avif"
/>
```

The build emits a 1200-pixel AVIF, sets `width` and `height` attributes so the browser reserves space before the image loads — the end of layout shift — and passes through the alt text untouched. Local imports know their intrinsic dimensions automatically; remote images can pass explicit dimensions or use `inferSize` to measure them at build. The `public` directory still exists for images you want served byte-for-byte, but anything that would benefit from resizing belongs in `src/assets`. Coming from a hand-rolled image pipeline — a script, a Makefile, a shrine to `srcset` — this replaces the whole shrine with one component.

## Responsive images

One image per `<img>` is a fixed cost charged to every device; responsive images let the browser choose. Pass `widths` and `sizes`, and the build produces multiple variants while the component emits the `srcset` and `sizes` attributes:

```astro
---
import { Image, Picture } from "astro:assets";
import hero from "../assets/harbor.jpg";
---
<Image
  src={hero}
  alt="Harbor at dawn"
  widths={[400, 800, 1600]}
  sizes="(min-width: 800px) 800px, 100vw"
/>

<Picture src={hero} alt="Harbor at dawn" formats={["avif", "webp"]} />
```

The phone downloads the 400-pixel variant; the desktop grabs the 1600; the `sizes` expression is you telling the browser how wide the image will render, which is what lets it choose well. When you want format fallbacks — AVIF where supported, WebP beneath it, JPEG under that — the `Picture` component layers them with a `<source>` per format.

The discipline that makes this worth it: art-direction decisions belong here, at the component, where the layout context lives. A hero banner and a thumbnail of the same source image are two `Image` calls with different `widths`, not two pre-exported files drifting out of sync in a folder.

## Fonts without the jank

Fonts have their own failure mode: the text is ready, the font is not, and the browser decides whether to show nothing or the wrong font. The fix is a stack of three small deliberate choices — self-host the files, preload the one that matters, and swap rather than block:

```astro
---
// src/layouts/BaseLayout.astro — preload link plus the font sheet
---
<head>
  <link rel="preload" href="/fonts/newsreader-400.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin />
</head>
<style is:global>
  @font-face {
    font-family: "Newsreader";
    src: url("/fonts/newsreader-400.woff2") format("woff2");
    font-weight: 400;
    font-display: swap;
  }
</style>
```

`font-display: swap` renders the fallback immediately and swaps when the real font lands — the visitor reads your words on the first paint, and the typeface refines a beat later. Preload only the one or two weights a first paint actually needs. Subset the files (a Latin subset of a body font is typically a third of the full size), and self-host rather than loading from a third-party origin — one fewer DNS lookup and no extra privacy policy. Astro's experimental Fonts API automates this pattern, but the mechanics above are stable, portable, and worth knowing regardless of which layer does the work.

## Key takeaways

- Component `<style>` blocks are scoped per file by generated attributes, so class names survive to devtools while collisions become impossible.
- Scoped styles cannot reach child or island markup by design; `:global(...)` for one selector or `is:global` for a block is the explicit opt-out.
- The cascade still applies: equal-specificity ties go to the later rule in bundle order, and the scoping attribute adds a class-level specificity bump.
- Tailwind 4 installs as a Vite plugin with a single `@import` — the `@astrojs/tailwind` integration is the legacy Tailwind 3 path.
- `astro:assets` Image and Picture components resize, reformat, and set dimensions at build; `widths` plus `sizes` is how responsive images stop overcharging phones.
- Self-host fonts, preload the first-paint weight, and use `font-display: swap` so text paints immediately and type arrives when it arrives.

Pages now look right and load fast — but navigation still costs a full page load, and forms still cost a round trip. The client router and actions fix both without turning the site into an SPA.

Two gaps have been left open on purpose. Navigation costs a full page load — fast, but perceptible. And forms do the most honest thing on the web: a round trip, a fresh page, and whatever the server says. This chapter closes both gaps without reneging on Chapter 1's deal. The *client router* intercepts navigations and swaps pages in place, with animated transitions and state that survives. *Actions* turn form posts into typed, validated server functions. Neither is a runtime you live inside — they are two thin upgrades to a multi-page site, and each degrades to the plain web behavior underneath if JavaScript never loads.

## What the client router buys you

A multi-page navigation is: browser requests a URL, receives a whole new document, throws away the old one, repaints everything. It is robust and it is stateless, and for most content sites it is fast enough that nobody complains — which is why Astro's default has no client router at all. The upgrade matters when full repaints are noticeable: a header that flickers, a scroll position that resets on a filtered list, a page-to-page flow that should feel like one product.

The client router changes the mechanics, not the model. Navigations are intercepted, the next page's HTML is fetched, and the DOM is swapped — the browser never builds an app, because there is no app; there is a sequence of documents, brokered smoothly. Everything stays a real URL that prefetches, caches, and works with JavaScript disabled, where the fallback is the ordinary full page load you had before. This is the "MPA renaissance" from Chapter 1 in shipped form: the browser keeps its architecture, and you borrow back the parts of SPA navigation people actually miss.

## `<ClientRouter />` and the navigation lifecycle

The router is opt-in, one component in the layout head:

```astro
---
// src/layouts/BaseLayout.astro
import { ClientRouter } from "astro:transitions";
---
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>{title}</title>
  <ClientRouter />
</head>
```

From then on, same-origin link clicks go through the router. The lifecycle emits events you can hook, and one matters above the rest: after each swap the DOM is new, so any page-level setup you used to do on load needs re-running:

```astro
<script>
  document.addEventListener("astro:after-swap", () => {
    // the old document is gone: re-announce the page view
    window.dataLayer?.push({ event: "pageview", path: location.pathname });
  });
</script>
```

The full sequence runs `astro:before-preparation`, `astro:after-preparation` (new page fetched), `astro:before-swap`, `astro:after-swap` (DOM replaced). Scripts behave sensibly: module scripts execute once per site lifetime, so this listener — like the deduplicated scripts from Chapter 5 — survives across navigations precisely because it registered once and listens on `document`. Analytics is the canonical use, and forgetting the `after-swap` hook is the canonical bug: a site that looks instrumented and reports exactly one page view.

## transition:animate and transition:name

Swapped pages don't have to hard-cut. The `transition:animate` directive animates any element across a navigation — built-ins include `fade`, `slide`, and `none`:

```astro
---
import { slide } from "astro:transitions";
---
<article transition:animate={slide({ duration: "0.2s" })}>
  <h1>Each article slides in, briefly</h1>
</article>
```

The more interesting directive is `transition:name`, which pairs elements *across pages*. Give the post title on the listing page and the post title on the detail page the same name, and the browser morphs one into the other during navigation — the shared-element transition that made native apps feel expensive:

```astro
<!-- listing page -->
<h2 transition:name={`title-${post.id}`}><a href={`/blog/${post.id}`}>{post.data.title}</a></h2>

<!-- detail page -->
<h1 transition:name={`title-${post.id}`}>{post.data.title}</h1>
```

Names must be unique per page, and template-literal names keyed on the item's id are the pattern that scales — one dynamic route, one name per entry. Under the hood these directives compile to the View Transitions API, so the animation is the browser's own machinery, skipped gracefully where the API is missing.

## State that survives with transition:persist

A swap replaces the DOM, which raises the question of what should *not* be replaced: the audio player that must keep playing, the sidebar search box holding its query, an island with state the user has arranged. `transition:persist` marks an element — most usefully an island — as the survivor:

```astro
---
import AudioPlayer from "../components/AudioPlayer.svelte";
---
<AudioPlayer
  client:load
  transition:persist="podcast-player"
  src="/episodes/42.mp3"
/>
```

When navigating between two pages that both render an element with that persist key, the router keeps the existing instance and swaps everything around it. The island keeps its runtime state — Svelte state included, since the component itself is never torn down — and a playing episode keeps playing. Without the directive, the same markup would hydrate fresh on every page: correct, but the kind of correctness that makes users close the tab. Persist is also the answer to a question the SPA crowd asks about MPAs: no, the page does not reload — the parts you persist don't, and the parts you don't are fresh documents, which is the trade working as designed.

## Forms the Astro way

A form is a POST to a URL, and the web's default form experience is: submit, wait, receive a new page. That default is *good* — it works without JavaScript, it survives slow phones, and it is the baseline any enhancement must preserve. Astro's actions API keeps the baseline and upgrades the tooling. Define an action on the server:

```ts
// src/actions/index.ts
import { defineAction, ActionError } from "astro:actions";
import { z } from "astro:schema";

export const server = {
  newsletter: defineAction({
    input: z.object({
      email: z.string().email(),
    }),
    handler: async (input, context) => {
      const ok = await addSubscriber(input.email);
      if (!ok) {
        throw new ActionError({
          code: "BAD_REQUEST",
          message: "The list is temporarily unavailable.",
        });
      }
      return { subscribed: true };
    },
  }),
};
```

The form binds to it directly, and this is the progressive-enhancement trick worth naming: with JavaScript, you get smooth handling; without it, the form is a plain POST to an endpoint Astro generates — where SvelteKit hands you `use:enhance` to layer this on, Astro's no-JS path *is* the native path, and everything else sweetens it:

```astro
---
// src/pages/subscribe.astro
import { actions } from "astro:actions";
---
<form method="POST" action={actions.newsletter}>
  <label>
    Email <input type="email" name="email" required />
  </label>
  <button>Subscribe</button>
</form>
```

## Actions: define, call, validate

The action above is half the API; the other half is calling it. From client-side code, actions are typed functions — the input type comes from the zod schema, the result type from the handler's return:

```ts
import { actions } from "astro:actions";

const { data, error } = await actions.newsletter({ email: "reader@example.com" });
if (error) {
  // error.code: "BAD_REQUEST", error.message from the ActionError
}
```

Validation is the schema's job: a malformed payload is rejected before `handler` runs, so the handler trusts `input.email` the way Rust code trusts a parsed type — the boundary does the checking, the interior does the work. `ActionError` is how a handler reports failure with a code the client can branch on; anything thrown unwrapped becomes a generic server error, so wrap the failures you expect and let the ones you don't stay loud.

For the no-JS form path, the result of a submission is available on the re-rendered page:

```astro
---
// src/pages/subscribe.astro
import { actions } from "astro:actions";

const result = Astro.getActionResult(actions.newsletter);
---
{result?.error && <p role="alert">{result.error.message}</p>}
{result?.data && <p>Check your inbox to confirm.</p>}
<form method="POST" action={actions.newsletter}>
  <!-- ... -->
</form>
```

`Astro.getActionResult` is present only when that form just posted to this page — error or success, the server renders the outcome, no client state required. The division of labor across the book now closes: pages render (Chapters 2 and 3), content is typed and queryable (Chapter 4), rendering modes choose where code runs (Chapter 6), and actions are the typed, validated contract at the POST boundary between them.

## Where SPAs still win

Honesty requires the boundary. The client router makes document navigation *feel* continuous, but a genuinely app-shaped product — one screen, hundreds of state transitions, optimistic everything, offline-first sync — is still better served by an SPA framework, for the same reason Chapter 1 gave: when every route is a live view, you want a runtime that owns the view, not a broker between documents. The gap the router closes is precisely the gap between "docs site" and "docs site where navigation doesn't flicker."

The mature pattern is a spectrum, and Astro holds both ends. Content pages: static, zero JS, client router smoothing the edges. App-shaped corners: islands with `transition:persist` surviving navigation, actions handling their mutations, endpoints feeding them data. And when a product outgrows the document model entirely — the dashboard case from Chapter 1 — the stack you built here exports cleanly: the actions and endpoints remain a typed API, and the SPA framework of your choice consumes them. You are not choosing a religion; you are placing each screen on the static-to-app spectrum, with the tools to move it later.

## Key takeaways

- The client router is opt-in via `<ClientRouter />` and turns navigations into DOM swaps while keeping real URLs, prefetching, and a no-JS fallback.
- `astro:after-swap` is the event that matters most: the DOM is new after every navigation, so page-view reporting and other document-level setup must re-run there.
- `transition:name` morphs elements across pages and `transition:animate` styles the swap; `transition:persist` keeps islands — and their state — alive across navigations.
- Actions pair a zod `input` schema with a typed `handler`; validation happens at the boundary, failures surface as `ActionError` with a code, and forms bind with `action={actions.newsletter}`.
- The no-JS form path is native, not degraded: `Astro.getActionResult` renders the outcome on the server, which is progressive enhancement without a `use:enhance` layer.
- SPAs still win for state-heavy, single-screen products — and the actions and endpoints you built here remain a typed API when a screen outgrows the document model.

One chapter remains: the ecosystem that wraps all of this — docs themes, integrations, adapters — and the production checklist for shipping it.

Eight chapters built the mental model: HTML first, islands where they pay, content with a schema, rendering as a dial. What remains is everything around the code — the integrations that save you from writing the boring half of every site, the docs theme that makes you look more professional than you feel, and the pipeline that turns `git push` into a deployed, measured, monitored site. This chapter tours the ecosystem, then assembles the production checklist. Launch is not a vibe; it is a list, and the list is short because Astro made most of the hard items default behavior.

## The ecosystem map

Astro's core is deliberately small, and the ecosystem carries the rest through *integrations* — packages that hook into the build to add a capability. The catalog covers the predictable ground: `@astrojs/mdx` (already load-bearing in Chapter 4), sitemap generation, icons, image tooling, and the framework integrations from Chapter 5. Installing one is a one-liner that edits your config for you:

```bash
npx astro add mdx sitemap partytown
```

That command installs the packages, wires them into `astro.config.mjs`, and tells you what it changed — the closest thing the JavaScript ecosystem has to `cargo add` behaving predictably. The mental model to hold: the core renders and routes; integrations extend the build; adapters (Chapter 6) teach the output to run somewhere. When you evaluate a third-party capability, the first question is which of those three shapes it takes, because it determines whether the feature costs runtime bytes or build-time work — the same budget discipline the whole book has applied, now applied to the ecosystem.

## Starlight for documentation

Documentation is the use case Astro's ecosystem handles best, and *Starlight* is the reason: a documentation theme that generates a full docs site from content collections. You already know the machinery underneath — your markdown becomes a collection, the schema gains docs-specific fields, and the theme supplies the rest:

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from "astro/config";
import starlight from "@astrojs/starlight";

export default defineConfig({
  integrations: [
    starlight({
      title: "Orbit Docs",
      sidebar: [
        { label: "Getting started", slug: "getting-started" },
        { label: "API", autogenerate: { directory: "api" } },
      ],
      locales: {
        en: { label: "English" },
        de: { label: "Deutsch" },
      },
    }),
  ],
});
```

What the theme brings is everything you would otherwise build badly: sidebar navigation with autogenerated sections, internationalization with translated UI strings, keyboard-accessible search (Pagefind, indexing the built site client-side), code blocks with copy buttons, and next/prev pagination. The value proposition is the one `cargo doc` taught the Rust world: documentation that is cheap to maintain stays maintained. Starlight costs an afternoon to stand up and repays it the first time someone finds the answer via built-in search instead of asking you.

## Integrations worth knowing

Beyond Starlight, a short list earns its keep on almost every content site. `@astrojs/sitemap` generates a `sitemap-index.xml` from your routes at build — set `site` in the config and forget it. RSS is a small helper library away, and a changelog that offers a feed is a changelog people actually follow. Icons ship through `astro-icon`, which inlines SVGs from an icon set at build time with zero client cost. And partytown, met in Chapter 5, belongs in the config of any site with third-party analytics:

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { defineConfig } from "astro/config";
import sitemap from "@astrojs/sitemap";

export default defineConfig({
  site: "https://example.com",
  integrations: [sitemap()],
});
```

The selection principle from the ecosystem map applies down the list: prefer integrations that work at build time, because they keep the zero-JS budget intact. A sitemap generated during the build costs nothing at request time; a client-side sitemap generator would be absurd, and most categories have both kinds of option. Read the integration's mechanism, not just its feature list — the good ones are the ones you cannot detect in the network tab.

## Performance budgets and audits

Astro's defaults put you near the top of the Lighthouse leaderboard, but "fast by default" is not a budget — a budget is a number you refuse to cross, written down before launch. The honest version is three lines: total page weight, JavaScript bytes, and Largest Contentful Paint on a throttled connection. Islands make the JavaScript line easy to reason about, because each island's cost is visible and local; images are the line that drifts, which is why Chapter 7's `astro:assets` pipeline is worth its setup cost. Measure with Lighthouse in Chrome, and enforce with Lighthouse CI so regressions arrive as failed checks rather than vibes:

```yaml
# lighthouserc.yml
ci:
  collect:
    numberOfRuns: 3
    staticDistDir: ./dist
  assert:
    assertions:
      categories:performance: ["error", { minScore: 0.9 }]
      total-byte-weight: ["error", { maxNumericValue: 500000 }]
```

Run against `dist` — the static output makes that possible — with multiple runs to smooth variance. The assertions fail the pipeline when the budget breaks, which converts performance from a launch-week panic into a gate that runs on every change. A budget you enforce in CI is a promise; one you check "sometimes" is a mood.

## Testing an Astro site

Testing splits along the rendering model. Pure functions — the utilities in `src/lib`, schema transformations, query filters — are ordinary TypeScript, and vitest covers them without any Astro involvement:

```ts
// src/lib/search.test.ts
import { describe, expect, it } from "vitest";
import { searchPosts } from "./search";

describe("searchPosts", () => {
  it("matches titles case-insensitively", () => {
    const results = searchPosts(fixtures, "ASTRO");
    expect(results.map((r) => r.id)).toContain("islands-explained");
  });
});
```

The pages themselves are best tested as a built site: run `astro build && astro preview`, and let Playwright drive a real browser against it. This catches what unit tests cannot — that `getStaticPaths` produced the routes, that the layout wires the navigation, that the island hydrates:

```ts
// tests/blog.spec.ts
import { test, expect } from "@playwright/test";

test("a published post renders with its byline", async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto("/blog/islands-explained");
  await expect(page.getByRole("heading", { level: 1 })).toBeVisible();
  await expect(page.getByText("Rin")).toBeVisible();
});

test("the newsletter form validates server-side", async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto("/subscribe");
  await page.getByLabel("Email").fill("not-an-email");
  await page.getByRole("button", { name: "Subscribe" }).click();
  await expect(page.getByRole("alert")).toBeVisible();
});
```

The second test is Chapter 8 proving itself with JavaScript deliberately left to Playwright's defaults — the action validated the input server-side, and the alert the user sees is rendered HTML. Keep the unit suite fast and the e2e suite small: a dozen well-chosen paths through the built site beats a hundred fragile ones.

## CI and deployment

Deployment splits by output mode, and the split is clean. Static output deploys as files — Netlify, Cloudflare Pages, GitHub Pages, any CDN — with the build command `npm run build` and the output directory `dist`. Request-time rendering needs the matching adapter from Chapter 6, and the host then runs the server output for you. Either way, the pipeline is the same shape:

```yaml
# .github/workflows/deploy.yml
name: Deploy
on:
  push:
    branches: [main]
jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with:
          node-version: 22
          cache: npm
      - run: npm ci
      - run: npm run build
        env:
          CMS_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.CMS_TOKEN }}
      - uses: actions/upload-pages-artifact@v3
        with:
          path: ./dist
```

The env wiring is Chapter 6 closing the loop: secrets live in the CI provider's secret store, reach the build as environment variables, and never sit in a committed `.env`. Two habits worth adopting early: run `astro check` and the test suite in the same job as the build, so a type error blocks deploy rather than shipping; and let the host's deploy previews render every pull request, because a broken layout caught in a preview costs a comment, not a rollback.

## The launch checklist

The list, in the order that catches the most problems per minute spent. It is short, and that is the point — each line is cheap because the defaults were right:

- Set `site` in `astro.config.mjs`, verify the sitemap and canonical URLs, and confirm `404.astro` renders like a real page rather than a shrug.
- Run Lighthouse on the three heaviest pages, confirm the budget holds, and check the built HTML for islands that hydrate but never needed to.
- Exercise one form end to end with JavaScript disabled — the action path from Chapter 8 — because your most important visitors will include at least one browser you did not test.
- Wire the analytics `astro:after-swap` event if the client router is on, or accept that the numbers undercount by every navigation after the first.
- Point the CMS's deploy webhook at the pipeline, so content changes rebuild the site without a human remembering to.

Launch is the loop closing where Chapter 1 opened it: a page is a document, rendered once, served everywhere, with JavaScript admitted only where it earns its place. The same discipline suits how you already work — Svelte components from your other projects drop into these pages as islands unchanged, and the Rust and Tauri toolchain is waiting when a product outgrows the browser entirely. Ship it.

## Key takeaways

- The ecosystem splits into build-time integrations, runtime islands, and adapters — and build-time options keep the zero-JS budget that Chapter 1 established.
- Starlight turns your content-collection knowledge into a full docs site with search, i18n, and navigation for an afternoon of setup.
- Write budgets as CI assertions — Lighthouse score and total byte weight enforced on every change — not as launch-week intentions.
- Test in two layers: vitest for the pure logic, Playwright against `build && preview` for the routes, layouts, and server-validated forms.
- Static output deploys anywhere as files; request-time output deploys with its adapter; either way, secrets enter through CI env, never committed files.
- The launch checklist is short because the defaults were right — and the mental model holds: documents first, islands where they pay, shipped.

Chapter 1 left you with the claim that Svelte compiles reactivity into your components. This chapter makes good on it. The goal is not API memorization — the runes fit on an index card — but a precise mental model of what happens when you declare, read, and write reactive values. Every later chapter assumes you have it: props, effects, bindings, and shared state are all this one machine wearing different hats.

## The signal underneath

Every modern frontend framework has converged on the same primitive, usually called a *signal*: a value paired with a list of whoever is currently reading it. When code reads the signal inside a reactive context — a render, a template, an effect — that reader is registered as a subscriber. When the value is written, the subscribers are notified and update themselves.

```js
// the primitive, stripped of polish: a value plus its readers
function signal(value) {
  const readers = new Set();
  return {
    get() {
      return value; // a real implementation registers the reader here
    },
    set(next) {
      value = next;
      readers.forEach((read) => read()); // notify and let each rerun
    }
  };
}
```

React hides this machinery inside hooks and the reconciler; Vue builds it from proxies; Svelte 5 compiles it directly into your code. When you write `let count = $state(0)`, the compiler turns `count` into a signal and routes every read and write through it. There is no store object to import and no subscription to manage — the variable *is* the reactive value. Hold that picture; the rest of the chapter is just its consequences.

## $state: declaring what changes

The `$state` rune declares a value that can change over time and whose changes the UI should follow:

```svelte
<script>
  let count = $state(0);
  let name = $state('Ada');

  function increment() {
    count += 1; // a write — every reader of count is notified
  }
</script>

<button onclick={increment}>
  {name} clicked {count} times
</button>
```

Two habits worth forming immediately. First, `$state` is a compiler keyword, not an import — you never write `import { state } from 'svelte'`, because the rune exists to be rewritten at build time. Second, do not declare everything as state. Values that never change should stay plain `const`s; state is a promise that something varies and a price paid in tracking machinery. The discipline of declaring only what changes is what makes a Svelte codebase easy to read: the `$state` calls are a map of everything that can move.

## Objects, arrays, and deep proxies

When you hand `$state` an object or array, the compiler wraps it in a *deep proxy* — reads and writes on any nested property are tracked and trigger updates:

```svelte
<script>
  let todo = $state({ title: 'write chapter', done: false });
  let tags = $state(['svelte', 'runes']);

  function finish() {
    todo.done = true; // plain mutation — and it just works
    tags.push('reactivity');
  }
</script>

<p class:done={todo.done}>{todo.title}</p>
<p>{tags.join(', ')}</p>
```

There is no immutability ceremony here. `todo.done = true` and `tags.push(...)` update the UI, because the proxy traps the mutation and notifies the readers. The wrapping is deep, so `state.user.address.city = 'Oslo'` is tracked too, and `Map` and `Set` are proxied with their methods intact. Two caveats keep you out of trouble: `$state.snapshot(todo)` returns a plain, non-proxied copy when you need one — for logging, `structuredClone`, or sending to a server — and the proxy is the reactive thing, which is exactly why the next and last sections matter.

## $derived: state that computes itself

Most state in an app is not independent; it is a function of other state. The `$derived` rune declares that relationship once, and the compiler maintains it forever:

```svelte
<script>
  let count = $state(2);
  let doubled = $derived(count * 2);
  let quadrupled = $derived(doubled * 2);
</script>

<p>{count} doubled is {doubled}, quadrupled is {quadrupled}</p>
```

A derived value recalculates when the values it reads change, and only then — until a dependency changes, the previous result is cached and re-reads are free. Deriveds can depend on other deriveds, forming a dependency graph the compiler traces automatically; there are no dependency arrays to maintain and no stale-closure hazards, because tracking is based on what the expression actually reads at runtime. The habit that separates clean Svelte code from tangled Svelte code is reaching for `$derived` instead of a second `$state`: if a value can be *computed*, it should never be *stored*, because stored copies eventually disagree with their source.

## $derived.by for multi-line logic

`$derived` takes a single expression, and some computations do not fit in one. The `$derived.by` rune takes a function instead, which unlocks statements — guards, loops, early returns:

```svelte
<script>
  let cart = $state([
    { name: 'USB cable', price: 9 },
    { name: 'keyboard', price: 79 }
  ]);
  let shipping = $state(5);

  let total = $derived.by(() => {
    const subtotal = cart.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.price, 0);
    return subtotal > 50 ? subtotal : subtotal + shipping;
  });
</script>

<p>total: {total}</p>
```

The semantics are identical to `$derived`: dependencies are whatever the function reads, caching works the same, and the result is read-only — assigning to `total` is a compile-time error, which is the point. A good refactoring rhythm is to start with a `$derived` expression and graduate to `$derived.by` the moment you need a `reduce` or a conditional; if the function keeps growing past a screen, it usually wants to be a plain helper function that takes values as arguments, called from inside the rune.

## Reassignment vs mutation

Svelte's reactivity answers an old question — does updating this value notify the UI? — with a simple rule: writes through `$state` do, always. `count += 1`, `cart.push(item)`, and `todo.done = true` all trigger updates, because the compiler routes every write through the signal or proxy. Reassignment and mutation are equally valid styles; use whichever reads best.

Two distinctions are still worth being precise about. For plain values — numbers, strings, booleans — reassignment is the only way to write, since there is nothing to mutate. For proxied containers, mutation is idiomatic and deep. And the old Svelte 4 workarounds are gone: there is no need for the `items = items` self-assignment trick to nudge the compiler, because runes-era reactivity tracks mutation directly. What has *not* changed is what "write" means at a distance — you cannot reassign someone else's `$state` binding across module boundaries, a constraint with real design consequences that Chapter 6 takes up when state moves out of components entirely.

## Signals are not variables

The model has one sharp edge, and it cuts people moving from other frameworks: a signal is a *binding*, not a value, so anything that severs the binding severs the reactivity. Destructuring is the classic case:

```svelte
<script>
  let user = $state({ name: 'Ada', admin: false });

  // wrong: name and admin are snapshots — they will never update
  // let { name, admin } = user;

  $effect(() => {
    console.log(user.name); // reruns whenever name changes
  });
</script>

<p>{user.name}</p>
```

The destructured `name` would be an ordinary string copied out of the proxy at that moment; changes to `user.name` would leave it stale. The fix is to read through the live object — in templates, in deriveds, in effects. The same logic explains why passing a `$state` value into a function passes the current value, why spreading an object into a new one loses tracking, and why the compiler's error messages will occasionally refuse to let you destructure reactive things. Reactivity lives in the reference; keep the reference and you keep the updates.

## Key takeaways

- `$state` is a compiler keyword that turns a variable into a signal — a value plus a subscriber list — so reads register and writes notify, with no subscription plumbing.
- Objects and arrays become deep proxies: mutation is tracked at any depth, and `$state.snapshot` gives you the plain copy when you need one.
- `$derived` (and `$derived.by` for multi-line logic) declares computed state with automatic dependency tracking and caching; prefer it over storing a second copy of anything computable.
- Both reassignment and mutation trigger updates; the Svelte 4 tricks for coercing reactivity are obsolete.
- Signals are bindings, not values — destructuring, spreading, or copying at the wrong moment freezes a snapshot, so read through the live reference.

The next question is how values cross component boundaries: `$props`, and the snippets that make children composable.

State, from Chapter 2, lives inside a component. Almost nothing interesting stays there: real interfaces are trees of components exchanging values downward and notifications upward. Svelte 5's answer is deliberately small — one rune for inputs, one construct for composable markup, and ordinary functions for events. This chapter covers the whole surface of the component contract, because once it is precise, everything from design systems to form libraries becomes legible.

## The component contract

A Svelte component is a `.svelte` file you import and use like an HTML element. The contract has three directions: *props* flow in from parent to child, *callbacks* flow out from child to parent, and *children* are passed in as markup to render. That is the entire protocol — there is no context object to master before you can wire two components together.

```svelte
<!-- App.svelte -->
<script>
  import Rating from './Rating.svelte';
  let score = $state(4);
</script>

<Rating value={score} max={5} onSelect={(next) => (score = next)} />
```

Note the shape of that call. `value` and `max` are data flowing down; `onSelect` is a function the child will call when something happens. If you have written React, the direction is familiar; if you have written Svelte 4, the difference will be — the dispatcher is gone, and we will get to that honestly in a moment. First, the receiving end.

## $props with TypeScript

A component declares everything it accepts by destructuring from `$props()`:

```svelte
<!-- Rating.svelte -->
<script lang="ts">
  import type { Snippet } from 'svelte';

  interface Props {
    value?: number;
    max?: number;
    label?: string;
    onSelect?: (value: number) => void;
    children?: Snippet;
  }

  let { value = 0, max = 5, label = 'rating', onSelect, children }: Props = $props();
</script>
```

That destructuring is the entire props API. Defaults sit inline where a reader expects them, required props are the ones without defaults, and TypeScript support comes from annotating the destructuring pattern with an interface — the compiler checks both sides of every usage, so a typo like `onselect` in the parent is caught at build time. The `children?: Snippet` entry anticipates the next section: children are just another prop, with a type of their own. Because `$props()` is destructured once at the top of the script, a component's public surface is always visible in one place — there is nowhere for an undocumented prop to hide.

## Rest props and spreading

Wrapper components constantly need to say "take everything I did not name and forward it." The rest pattern does exactly that, and spreading passes it along:

```svelte
<!-- Button.svelte -->
<script lang="ts">
  import type { HTMLButtonAttributes } from 'svelte/elements';

  interface Props extends HTMLButtonAttributes {
    variant?: 'primary' | 'ghost';
  }

  let { variant = 'primary', class: className = '', children, ...rest }: Props = $props();
</script>

<button class={`btn ${variant} ${className}`} {...rest}>
  {@render children?.()}
</button>
```

Three details repay attention. The rest pattern `...rest` collects every prop the component did not destructure — including `disabled`, `aria-label`, and any `onclick` a caller passes — so a wrapper stays transparent instead of becoming a chokepoint. `class` is renamed during destructuring because it is not a legal identifier on its own. And `interface Props extends HTMLButtonAttributes` borrows the DOM's own attribute types from `svelte/elements`, so your button accepts everything a real button does, with types, for free. This extend-and-forward pattern is the backbone of most component libraries.

## Children and snippets

Children — the markup between a component's tags — arrive as a prop named `children`, and you render it where it belongs with `{@render children()}`:

```svelte
<!-- Card.svelte -->
<script lang="ts">
  import type { Snippet } from 'svelte';

  let { title, children }: { title: string; children: Snippet } = $props();
</script>

<article class="card">
  <h2>{title}</h2>
  <div class="body">{@render children()}</div>
</article>
```

```svelte
<!-- App.svelte -->
<Card title="Release notes">
  <p>Everything the compiler fixed this week.</p>
</Card>
```

The mechanism is the *snippet*: a reusable chunk of template that compiles to a function. `children` is the implicit snippet a parent writes between your tags, and `{@render children()}` is the explicit call — including the parentheses, because you are invoking it, wherever and however many times you like. If children are optional, type the prop as `Snippet | undefined` and render with `{@render children?.()}`. If you met Svelte 4, this replaces the `<slot />` element entirely: snippets are values, so they can be stored, passed as props, and rendered conditionally, which slots could never do.

## Snippets with parameters

Snippets can take arguments, which is how a component hands its caller the data to render — the job slot props used to do:

```svelte
<!-- Table.svelte -->
<script lang="ts">
  import type { Snippet } from 'svelte';

  interface Row {
    id: number;
    name: string;
    price: number;
  }

  let { rows, row }: { rows: Row[]; row: Snippet<[Row]> } = $props();
</script>

<table>
  {#each rows as item (item.id)}
    <tr>{@render row(item)}</tr>
  {/each}
</table>
```

The parent declares a named snippet with `{#snippet row(item)}`, and the type `Snippet<[Row]>` documents that it receives one `Row` per render:

```svelte
<!-- App.svelte -->
<Table rows={items}>
  {#snippet row(item)}
    <td>{item.name}</td>
    <td>{item.price.toFixed(2)}</td>
  {/snippet}
</Table>
```

The inversion is worth savoring: `Table` owns the looping and the markup of the row shell, while the caller owns the cells. Snippets close over their declaring scope, so `item` here plus any local state are both available inside — no render-prop indirection, no `let:` directives to remember.

## Callback props instead of events

In Svelte 5, an "event" from a child component is a prop whose name starts with `on` and whose value is a function. The child calls it; the parent decides what it means:

```svelte
<!-- SearchBox.svelte -->
<script lang="ts">
  interface Props {
    onSearch: (query: string) => void;
  }

  let { onSearch }: Props = $props();
  let query = $state('');

  function submit(event: SubmitEvent) {
    event.preventDefault();
    onSearch(query);
  }
</script>

<form onsubmit={submit}>
  <input bind:value={query} placeholder="search the docs" />
  <button>search</button>
</form>
```

The honesty first: Svelte 4's `createEventDispatcher` — with its `on:message` listener syntax — still exists but is deprecated, and new code should not use it. The replacement is a deliberate upgrade. Callback props are ordinary values, so they compose with the rest of the language: they are typed, they can be optional, they can be renamed or wrapped, and they show up in the same destructuring as every other prop. DOM events work the same way — Svelte 5 uses the platform's lowercase names, so you write `onclick` and `oninput` as attributes rather than `on:click` and `on:input` directives. One convention, everywhere.

## Composition patterns

Because snippets and callbacks are values, the composition patterns are the ones functions already taught you. A component can offer named snippets as props, each with a fallback rendered only when the caller omits it:

```svelte
<!-- Modal.svelte -->
<script lang="ts">
  import type { Snippet } from 'svelte';

  interface Props {
    title: string;
    header?: Snippet;
    footer?: Snippet;
    children: Snippet;
  }

  let { title, header, footer, children }: Props = $props();
</script>

<div class="modal" role="dialog" aria-label={title}>
  <header>
    {#if header}{@render header()}{:else}<h3>{title}</h3>{/if}
  </header>
  <div class="content">{@render children()}</div>
  {#if footer}<footer>{@render footer()}</footer>{/if}
</div>
```

Callers fill in only the parts they care about and can even build a snippet once and pass it to several components. The design discipline that falls out of this chapter: data down through props, decisions up through callbacks, structure in through snippets. A component that reaches outward any other way — global state read in a leaf, a parent reached into from a child — is a design smell we will have the tools to name by Chapter 6.

## Key takeaways

- The component contract is three directions: `$props()` in, callback props out, snippets in as renderable markup — no dispatcher, no slots.
- Destructuring `$props()` with an interface is the whole typing story: defaults inline, required props visible, errors caught on both sides.
- The rest pattern plus spreading keeps wrappers transparent, and `HTMLButtonAttributes`-style interfaces borrow the DOM's own types.
- `children` is a `Snippet` — a compiled function you render with `{@render children()}`, optionally with parameters typed as `Snippet<[Row]>`.
- Events are callback props named `onSomething`, and DOM events use the platform's lowercase names like `onclick`; `createEventDispatcher` is deprecated.

Props carry data down; next we look at the template syntax that decides what renders — conditionals, lists, and the two-way `bind:` wiring.

Chapters 2 through 4 covered values flowing into the UI. Some work, though, has to flow *outward*: setting the document title, drawing on a canvas, logging an analytics event, opening a socket. The `$effect` rune is the bridge from reactive state to the imperative world, and like any bridge it is best crossed on purpose. This chapter covers how effects run, how they clean up after themselves, where they do not belong, and how a component's whole life — mount, update, destroy — fits together.

## What an effect is for

An effect is a function that runs *after* the DOM has been updated, automatically re-runs when the reactive values it reads change, and cleans up after itself. That is the whole definition. The reason effects exist is asymmetry: your components are reactive, but most of the platform is not. The `document.title` is a string, a canvas context is a blob of imperative calls, an analytics endpoint is a side door out of your app. Something must re-run imperative code when state changes — and something must stop re-running it when the component that cares is gone.

The word *after* carries more weight than it seems. Because effects run once the DOM is settled, they are the correct place for code that must see the final rendered result — measuring an element, syncing a third-party widget, scrolling a list into view. Effects never run during server-side rendering, since there is no DOM to be after; they are browser-only by construction, which quietly removes a class of `typeof window` guards from your code.

## $effect: timing and dependencies

```svelte
<script>
  let count = $state(0);

  $effect(() => {
    document.title = `count is ${count}`;
  });
</script>

<button onclick={() => count++}>increment</button>
```

The effect reads `count`, so `count` is its dependency. The compiler does not need you to say so: tracking works the way Chapter 2 described — whatever the effect *synchronously reads* while running becomes something it subscribes to. When `count` changes, the effect re-runs after the DOM updates, batched with any other pending work. Three consequences are worth internalizing. Dependencies are discovered from actual reads, so there are no dependency arrays to keep honest, and no stale ones to mislead you. Asynchronous reads — a value read after an `await` — are *not* tracked, which is usually what you want for one-shot work. And because the effect runs after render, reading layout from it is safe in a way that mid-render reads are not.

## Cleanup functions

An effect can return a function, which runs before the effect re-runs and once more when its owner is destroyed. This is Rust's `Drop` wearing JavaScript: cleanup is attached to the thing that acquired the resource, not sprinkled at call sites.

```svelte
<script>
  let query = $state('');

  $effect(() => {
    const controller = new AbortController();
    fetch(`/api/suggest?q=${query}`, { signal: controller.signal });

    return () => controller.abort();
  });
</script>

<input bind:value={query} placeholder="type to search" />
```

Every keystroke changes `query`, the effect re-runs, and *before* it does, the previous run's cleanup aborts the in-flight request. The ordering guarantee — old cleanup, then new effect — means each run starts from a clean slate, and the final cleanup runs on destroy, so nothing outlives the component that started it. Timers, event listeners, subscriptions, and sockets all follow the same shape: acquire inside the effect, release in the returned function. If you find cleanup logic living anywhere else, that is usually the bug.

## Where effects don't belong

The most common effect bug in every signals-based framework is using an effect to compute state. It usually arrives as muscle memory from other libraries:

```svelte
<script>
  let count = $state(0);

  // the reflex: mirror state through an effect
  let doubled = $state(0);
  $effect(() => {
    doubled = count * 2;
  });
</script>
```

This works, in the way that shipping without tests works. It costs an extra render pass — `doubled` changes *after* the DOM updates, then triggers another update cycle. It hides the data flow: nothing about `doubled`'s declaration says where its value comes from. And it invites accidental loops, since effects both read and write state. The fix is Chapter 2's rune, applied with intent:

```svelte
<script>
  let count = $state(0);
  let doubled = $derived(count * 2);
</script>
```

`$derived` computes lazily, caches, and participates in the same render pass as everything else — no second cycle, and the dependency is declared at the site of the value. The rule of thumb that follows: **state changes should be computed with `$derived`; effects are for the world outside the reactive graph.** If an effect's only output is another piece of state, it is a derived wearing a trench coat, and the compiler will be happier when you arrest it.

## $effect.pre and untrack

Two modifiers refine the default timing. `$effect.pre` runs *before* the DOM updates — in the same window where Svelte itself computes what changed — which is the right moment to measure or record the current layout before Svelte moves anything:

```svelte
<script>
  let list = $state([]);
  let lastHeight = 0;

  $effect.pre(() => {
    lastHeight = document.body.scrollHeight;
  });
</script>
```

`untrack`, from the `svelte` module, is the opposite adjustment: it reads reactive values *without* subscribing, for the reads you do not want to trigger re-runs:

```svelte
<script>
  import { untrack } from 'svelte';

  let log = $state([]);

  $effect(() => {
    console.log(`log has ${log.length} entries`);
    const previous = untrack(() => log[log.length - 2]);
    console.log(`previous entry: ${previous}`);
  });
</script>
```

The effect re-runs when `log.length` changes, but not when the entry at `length - 2` happens to change on its own — the `untrack` read is deliberate, not accidental. Use it sparingly and locally: a whole effect wrapped in `untrack` is usually an effect that should not exist.

## onMount, and when you still need it

`onMount` registers a function that runs exactly once, after the component's first render:

```svelte
<script>
  import { onMount } from 'svelte';

  let rows = $state([]);

  onMount(async () => {
    const res = await fetch('/api/rows');
    rows = await res.json();
  });
</script>
```

It returns a cleanup function of its own, and — like effects — it never runs on the server. The distinction from `$effect` is re-run semantics: `onMount` runs once; `$effect` re-runs with its dependencies. That makes `onMount` the natural home for one-time setup that *reads nothing* or should never repeat — fetching initial data, instantiating a heavy library, wiring a listener that lives for the component's whole life. In SvelteKit, though, data fetching at mount is usually the wrong layer entirely: Chapter 8's `load` functions fetch before the component renders, on the server where possible. Keep `onMount` for genuinely client-only initialization, and it serves faithfully.

## The lifecycle of a component

Putting the pieces together, a component's life is: init — where `$props`, `$state`, and `$derived` are set up and effects are scheduled — then *mount*, where the DOM exists, `onMount` callbacks and then effects run; then a repeat cycle of *state change, DOM update, effect cleanup, effect re-run*; and finally *destroy*, where cleanups run in reverse order and the DOM is released. The framework guarantees the pairs: every listener acquired has its removal scheduled, every subscription has its unsubscribe, in code you write adjacent to the acquisition.

One last tool for completeness: `$effect.root` creates effects *outside* a component's initialization, returning a function that disposes them. It is the escape hatch for libraries, tests, and module-level code that needs reactive consumers with a manual off switch — most of the time you will never call it, but knowing it exists explains how runes can drive state outside `.svelte` files at all, which is precisely where Chapter 6 takes the story.

```js
// anywhere: effects with a manual off switch
import { $effect } from 'svelte';

const dispose = $effect.root(() => {
  $effect(() => console.log(theme.mode));
});

// later: dispose();
```

## Key takeaways

- `$effect` runs after DOM updates, auto-tracks the reactive values it synchronously reads, and never runs during server rendering.
- Return a cleanup function to release resources — it runs before each re-run and on destroy, pairing every acquisition with a release.
- Computing state in an effect is the classic misuse; if the output is state, the answer is `$derived`, and the extra render pass is the symptom.
- `$effect.pre` reads layout before Svelte updates the DOM; `untrack` reads without subscribing when a dependency would cause unwanted re-runs.
- `onMount` is for one-time, non-repeating setup — and in SvelteKit, initial data belongs in `load` instead; `$effect.root` extends effects beyond components.

Effects connect your state to the outside world. Next, we leave the component entirely: state that is shared, and the discipline that keeps it sane.

So far, every piece of state in this book has been born inside a component and died with it. That privacy is a feature — Chapter 3 argued that a component reaching outside itself is a design smell — but some state genuinely belongs to no single component: the current theme, the signed-in user, the contents of a cart. This chapter covers the three places shared state can live in Svelte 5, the failure mode that `.svelte.js` modules must be handled around, and an honest account of when the older store system is still the right tool.

## Why shared state is a discipline

The naive fix for prop drilling is to make state global, and the naive version of that fix is a plain module-level variable. It works until three things go wrong: the value changes and nothing re-renders, because a plain variable has no reactivity; every module that imports it can silently mutate it, because there is no boundary; and tests interfere with each other, because the "singleton" persists across them. SvelteKit adds a fourth: with server-side rendering, module state is shared across *requests*, so one user's data can leak into another's page if you are careless.

So treat shared state as a designed surface, not an accident: a module with a clear API, mutations that happen through declared functions, and a shape that survives being imported from anywhere. The runes make that design pleasant, and the rest of the chapter is the pattern library.

## State in .svelte.js modules

Runes are not confined to `.svelte` files. Any module whose name ends in `.svelte.js` (or `.svelte.ts`) is compiled with the same reactivity, so it can export live state:

```js
// src/lib/theme.svelte.js
export const theme = $state({
  mode: 'light',
  accent: 'orangered'
});

export function setMode(mode) {
  theme.mode = mode;
}
```

```svelte
<!-- anywhere: ThemeToggle.svelte -->
<script>
  import { theme, setMode } from '$lib/theme.svelte';
</script>

<button onclick={() => setMode(theme.mode === 'light' ? 'dark' : 'light')}>
  {theme.mode}
</button>
```

Every importer sees the same live object — the component reading `theme.mode` re-renders when `setMode` runs, no matter how far apart they are in the tree. Now the one sharp edge, and it matters: **you cannot export a reassignable `$state` directly.** A line like `export let count = $state(0)` looks reasonable and is not: when another module imports `count`, it receives the *current value*, a snapshot — not the live binding. Reassigning `count` inside the module later updates nothing anywhere, because ES module exports are read-only views, not references. The fix is to never export the state itself as a reassignable thing. Export an object and mutate its properties — proxies from Chapter 2 make that reactive — or export functions that do the mutating, as `setMode` does above. Both give importers a stable handle on something alive.

## Reactive classes

When shared state comes with invariants and operations, a class keeps them together. Svelte 5 treats `$state` and `$derived` as class fields:

```js
// src/lib/cart.svelte.js
export class Cart {
  items = $state([]);

  total = $derived(this.items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.price, 0));
  count = $derived(this.items.length);

  add(item) {
    this.items.push(item);
  }

  remove(id) {
    this.items = this.items.filter((item) => item.id !== id);
  }

  clear() {
    this.items = [];
  }
}
```

A `new Cart()` is a fully reactive object: the template reading `cart.total` re-renders when `items` changes, because the derived fields track the `$state` array exactly as Chapter 2 described. The class shape earns its keep when behavior and state belong together — validation in `add`, an `itemCount` cap, persistence hooks — and when you want more than one instance (a cart per session, a player per track) with types your editor can navigate. Instantiate once in a module and export the instance, or export the class and let each consumer own a copy; both patterns have their place, and the reactive fields work identically either way.

## The store contract

Before runes, Svelte's shared-state answer was the *store* — and it is worth knowing precisely, because it is a lesson in minimal API design. A store is any object with one method:

```js
// the entire contract: subscribe, and get an unsubscribe back
export function createCounter() {
  let value = 0;
  const subscribers = new Set();

  return {
    subscribe(run) {
      subscribers.add(run);
      run(value); // call immediately with the current value
      return () => subscribers.delete(run);
    },
    increment() {
      value += 1;
      subscribers.forEach((run) => run(value));
    }
  };
}
```

That is the whole protocol: `subscribe` takes a callback, calls it immediately with the current value, and returns an unsubscribe function. Anything obeying it is a store, and every part of the ecosystem — Svelte's own stores, RxJS observables via adapters, your hand-rolled objects — interoperates through it. The design wins over ad-hoc event emitters are the immediate first call (subscribers are never stale) and the returned cleanup (Chapter 5's acquire-release pairing, built into the contract). No framework object is required to satisfy it, which is why stores remain the lingua franca for publishing values from code that has never heard of Svelte.

## writable, readable, derived

Hand-rolling the contract teaches it; `svelte/store` ships the everyday versions. `writable` wraps a value with `set` and `update` methods; `readable` exposes no mutators at all, only a start function that runs when the first subscriber arrives; `derived` computes a store from other stores:

```js
// src/lib/stores.js
import { writable, readable, derived } from 'svelte/store';

export const count = writable(0);

export const secondsSinceOpen = readable(0, (set) => {
  const start = Date.now();
  const id = setInterval(() => set(Math.floor((Date.now() - start) / 1000)), 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(id);
});

export const doubled = derived(count, (value) => value * 2);
```

The `readable` start function is the contract's elegance in action: the interval only runs while someone is listening, and the returned cleanup fires when the last subscriber leaves — lazy resources for free. `derived` accepts several input stores and can itself return a cleanup for reactive-but-stateful derivations. Notice what these are not: they are not runes, so outside a component you read their current value with `get(store)`, and they do not benefit from compiler tracking. They are a runtime convention — portable, mature, and framework-agnostic in a way runes are not.

## The $store auto-subscription

Inside components, stores get one convenience that hides all the plumbing: prefix a store with `$` and Svelte subscribes on mount, unsubscribes on destroy, and treats the value as a reactive variable:

```svelte
<script>
  import { count, doubled } from '$lib/stores';
</script>

<button onclick={() => count.update((n) => n + 1)}>
  {count} doubled is {doubled}
</button>
```

Reading `$count` in the template or script subscribes; assigning to `$count = 5` compiles to `count.set(5)`. The subscription lifecycle is tied to the component exactly as Chapter 5 promised, and the `$`-values participate in reactivity like any `$state`. The `$` prefix works in `.svelte.js` modules too, which is how runes-era code and store-era libraries cooperate: a runes component can consume a third-party store with `$store` one day and wrap it in a reactive class the next. Interop runs in both directions, so neither system traps you.

## Choosing between runes and stores

The honest decision table, in prose. **Default to runes** — `$state` in a `.svelte.js` module, or a reactive class — for state your own app owns: they need less ceremony, compose with `$derived` and `$effect`, and keep one mental model across the codebase. **Reach for a store** when the value is produced outside your control — an RxJS stream, a WebSocket adapter, a library that publishes via `subscribe` — because the contract means zero glue; when a value has many independent consumers that come and go, where `readable`'s lazy-start semantics genuinely help; or when you are publishing state from a package that must not require Svelte 5 to use. There is one more SvelteKit-shaped caveat: module-level shared state of *any* kind is per-server, not per-user, so request-scoped values belong in `load` functions and `locals`, which is Chapter 8's territory. Choose the mechanism by who owns the value's lifecycle, not by fashion — and then keep mutations behind declared functions, whichever mechanism you picked.

## Key takeaways

- Shared state is a designed API, not a global variable: module state without reactivity, boundaries, or reset care causes silent bugs and cross-request leaks.
- Runes work in `.svelte.js` modules; export an object or mutations, never a reassignable `export let $state`, which importers see as a frozen snapshot.
- Classes with `$state` and `$derived` fields bundle invariants with the data they protect, and support multiple instances with full reactivity.
- A store is any object with `subscribe` returning an unsubscribe — the minimal contract that keeps the ecosystem interoperable.
- `writable`, `readable`, and `derived` cover the store shapes; `$store` auto-subscription bridges stores into components; default to runes and reach for stores at interop and multi-consumer boundaries.

State is settled. Chapter 7 adds motion — transitions and animations as template primitives rather than bolted-on libraries.

Most interfaces animate through a side door: a library takes a DOM node, measures it, and fights your framework's rendering cycle for control of the element. Svelte refuses the side door. Because the compiler knows exactly when an element enters and leaves the DOM — the `{#if}` and `{#each}` blocks from Chapter 4 — motion can be a template directive, attached to the element it belongs to, compiled into code that runs at precisely the right moment. This chapter covers the directives, the built-in effects, and the two motion values that round out the toolkit.

## Why transitions belong in the template

The problem with imperative animation is not the animation — it is the *lifecycle mismatch*. A library you call on mount has no idea that Chapter 4's `{#if}` block is about to destroy its target node, or that a list re-render is about to reorder it. So you end up writing guards, timers, and cancellation code whose only job is to reconcile the animator with the renderer.

Svelte deletes the mismatch by putting transitions where the lifecycle already lives. A directive like `transition:fade` tells the compiler: when this element enters the DOM, play this effect; when it leaves, play it out — and *defer the removal* until the exit finishes. Because the compiler generates the wiring, there is nothing to clean up, no node to capture with `bind:this`, and no orphaned animation. Most of the built-ins are expressed as CSS, so they run on the compositor and skip JavaScript frames entirely — motion that is declarative in the source and cheap at runtime.

## The transition, in, and out directives

`transition:` is the bidirectional form — the same effect plays forward on entry and in reverse on exit:

```svelte
<script>
  import { fade } from 'svelte/transition';
  let visible = $state(true);
</script>

<button onclick={() => (visible = !visible)}>toggle</button>

{#if visible}
  <p transition:fade>now you see me</p>
{/if}
```

The element fades in when the block appears and fades out when it disappears — and while the exit plays, the element stays in the DOM, so removal waits for the fade. When entry and exit should look different, the directional forms take over: `in:` specifies the entrance effect, `out:` the exit one, and they accept different effects or different parameters. A toast, for instance, might arrive with `in:fly={{ y: 16 }}` and leave with `out:fade`. The directives attach to any element inside a block that creates or destroys it — `{#if}`, `{#each}`, `{#key}`, `{#await}` — which is to say: wherever Chapter 4's blocks fire, transitions can play.

## The built-ins: fade, fly, blur, scale, slide

The `svelte/transition` module ships five effects that cover most interfaces:

```svelte
<script>
  import { fade, fly, blur, scale, slide } from 'svelte/transition';
  let open = $state(true);
</script>

{#if open}
  <div transition:fly={{ y: 16, duration: 200 }}>slides up while fading</div>
  <div transition:scale={{ start: 0.96 }}>grows from almost-right size</div>
  <div transition:blur={{ duration: 150 }}>unblurs into place</div>
  <details transition:slide>accordion content, height animated</details>
{/if}
```

Each earns its niche. `fade` is the workhorse for subtle presence changes. `fly` combines a fade with movement along an axis — toasts, dropdowns, anything entering from off-position. `scale` is the popover and dialog entrance. `blur` reads as "materializing" and pairs well with `fly`. `slide` animates height and margin, which makes it the honest choice for accordions and expanding rows — the DOM actually collapses, rather than being masked. All of them accept a parameter object, and all of them compose with the directional forms, so mixing `in:fly={{ y: 16 }}` with `out:fade` is a one-line art direction.

## Parameters and custom transitions

Every transition takes a parameters object as its value: `transition:fade={{ duration: 200 }}` runs the fade in 200 milliseconds. Common parameters include `duration`, `delay`, and `easing` — an easing function imported from `svelte/easing` that shapes the rate of change. When the built-ins run out, a transition is just a function:

```svelte
<script>
  import { cubicOut } from 'svelte/easing';

  function whoosh(node, params) {
    return {
      duration: params.duration ?? 300,
      easing: cubicOut,
      css: (t) => `opacity: ${t}; transform: translateX(${(1 - t) * 40}px)`
    };
  }
</script>

<p transition:whoosh={{ duration: 400 }}>custom, but CSS-driven</p>
```

The function receives the DOM node and the parameters from the template, and returns a descriptor. The `css` key is the important one: a function of `t`, from 0 to 1, returning style strings — and the compiler turns it into a real CSS keyframe animation, so the browser runs it on its own clock. If the effect genuinely cannot be expressed as styles, a `tick` function receives each intermediate `t` instead and manipulates the node in JavaScript, at the cost of running on the main thread. Prefer `css` whenever possible; reach for `tick` the way you reach for `requestAnimationFrame` — deliberately.

## Global versus local transitions

Here is a default that surprises people *into* good behavior: transitions are *local* by default, meaning a transition plays only when the block that immediately contains its element toggles. If a `{#if}` somewhere above the tree flips and re-renders a subtree, elements deep inside do not suddenly replay their entrances. The default exists because the opposite — Svelte 4's global behavior — produced mass flicker: flip one ancestor condition and a dozen items would animate as if they had all just arrived.

When you genuinely do want that — the classic case is a list whose items should animate whenever the list appears — the `|global` modifier opts in:

```svelte
{#if showList}
  {#each items as item (item.id)}
    <li transition:fade|global>{item.label}</li>
  {/each}
{/if}
```

The mental model: *local* means "I animate when *my* block changes," *global* means "I animate whenever anything above me changes." Read a template with that lens and the modifier stops being magic — it is a scoping decision, made visible at the element.

## Flip animations for list moves

Transitions cover entering and leaving; reordering inside a list is a third case, and it gets its own directive family: `animate:`, which works only on the immediate children of a keyed `{#each}` block:

```svelte
<script>
  import { flip } from 'svelte/animate';
  import { fade } from 'svelte/transition';
  let queue = $state([
    { id: 1, title: 'compile' },
    { id: 2, title: 'test' },
    { id: 3, title: 'ship' }
  ]);

  function promote() {
    queue = [queue[1], queue[0], ...queue.slice(2)];
  }
</script>

<button onclick={promote}>promote first</button>

{#each queue as item (item.id)}
  <li animate:flip={{ duration: 250 }} transition:fade>{item.title}</li>
{/each}
```

`flip` — *first, last, invert, play* — measures where a moving item was and where it lands, then animates the delta, so items glide to their new positions instead of teleporting. Combined with the keyed `{#each}` from Chapter 4 (the key is what lets the compiler recognize *the same item* across renders) and a `transition:` for the items that enter or leave mid-shuffle, you get list choreography that usually takes a dedicated animation library. Sortable lists, priority queues, drag-and-drop reorders — `flip` plus a key covers them all in one directive.

## spring and tweened values

The last two tools are not directives at all: `svelte/motion` exports *motion values* — reactive values that interpolate toward their target instead of jumping there:

```svelte
<script>
  import { tweened, spring } from 'svelte/motion';
  import { cubicOut } from 'svelte/easing';

  const progress = tweened(0, { duration: 400, easing: cubicOut });
  const gauge = spring(0, { stiffness: 0.15, damping: 0.6 });

  async function advance() {
    progress.current = Math.min(progress.current + 0.25, 1);
    gauge.current += 1;
  }
</script>

<progress value={progress.current}></progress>
<button onclick={advance}>advance</button>
```

Write to `.current` — Svelte 5's API, a deliberate change from the old call-the-store style — and the value animates toward the new target on every frame: `tweened` over a fixed duration with an easing curve, `spring` along a physical spring with `stiffness` and `damping`. Because they are reactive values, they slot into the machinery from Chapter 2: templates read them, deriveds compute from them, and — the payoff — SVG attributes, canvas draws, and styles all update per frame with zero animation code in your components. Motion values are state that happens to be continuous; the interface just reads smoother.

## Key takeaways

- Transitions are template directives tied to block lifecycle: the compiler wires entry, exit, and deferred removal, so there is nothing to clean up.
- `transition:` plays both directions; `in:` and `out:` split them; the five built-ins — `fade`, `fly`, `blur`, `scale`, `slide` — cover most needs with parameter objects.
- Custom transitions are functions returning a `css` interpolator, which the compiler emits as keyframes; reserve `tick` for genuinely non-CSS effects.
- Transitions are local by default, replaying only when their own block toggles; `|global` opts into ancestor-triggered replays.
- `animate:flip` animates reordering inside keyed `{#each}` blocks, and `svelte/motion`'s `tweened` and `spring` provide continuous reactive values — motion without an animation library.

The app is stateful, composed, and now animated. Chapter 8 is the frame around it: SvelteKit, where routes, data, and the server live.

The last mile of a frontend project is where the compiler mental model from Chapter 1 pays its second dividend. Svelte already moved rendering work to build time; shipping is mostly about making the *remaining* decisions deliberate — where the output runs, what renders before JavaScript arrives, which secrets stay private, what is tested, and who the interface excludes if you are careless. None of this is glamorous, and all of it is the difference between a demo and a product.

## What shipping means for a frontend

A frontend ships when four questions have answers. *Where does it run?* — decided by an adapter, from a static file host to a Node server. *When does rendering happen?* — at build time, at request time, or in the browser, per route. *What does the code know?* — which environment variables are safe to inline and which must stay server-side. *What happens when it is wrong?* — tests that catch regressions and an accessibility posture that treats warnings as defects. SvelteKit gives each question a small, honest configuration surface, and this chapter walks them in order, finishing with a checklist you can run before any launch.

## Adapters and deployment targets

An adapter translates SvelteKit's output into a specific host's expectations. You pick one in `svelte.config.js`, and — this is the point — everything else about your app stays the same:

```js
// svelte.config.js
import adapter from '@sveltejs/adapter-node';
import { vitePreprocess } from '@sveltejs/vite-plugin-svelte';

export default {
  preprocess: vitePreprocess(),
  kit: {
    adapter: adapter({ precompress: true })
  }
};
```

`adapter-auto`, the default, detects common platforms (Vercel, Netlify, Cloudflare) and picks the right output for you. `adapter-node` produces a self-contained server — the right choice for containers, self-hosting, or running beside a Rust binary, including inside a Tauri app's build where your SvelteKit assets sit alongside code you compile with Cargo. `adapter-static` emits plain files for a CDN, with one contract: every route must be prerenderable, which is the next section's subject. Swapping hosts is a config change plus a build, not a refactor — deploy target is a decision you make late and can revise.

## Prerendering and rendering strategies

By default, SvelteKit renders pages on the server at request time and hydrates them in the browser — the best of both worlds, at the cost of a server. Two flags per route, settable in `+page.js` or `+layout.js`, shift the work:

```js
// src/routes/+layout.js
export const prerender = true; // build HTML at build time
// export const ssr = false;   // or skip server rendering entirely (SPA)
```

Prerendering runs your pages once, during `npm run build`, and writes the HTML to disk: instant responses, trivial hosting, and SEO for free — ideal for marketing sites, docs, and blogs, which is why `adapter-static` requires it. SvelteKit crawls your links from an entry point and prerenders everything reachable. The `ssr = false` escape hatch disables server rendering for a subtree — occasionally right for dashboards that are pure client state from the first pixel — and costs you first-paint speed and indexability, so opt in per route rather than globally. The discipline is to decide per route, not per project: a product can prerender its homepage, server-render its catalog, and client-render its admin, all in one app.

## Environment variables and secrets

SvelteKit makes the public/private boundary a compile-time fact instead of a naming convention you hope people follow:

```ts
// build-time, inlined into the bundle — safe in any component
import { PUBLIC_API_URL } from '$env/static/public';

// server-only: importing this from client code is a build error
import { PRIVATE_STRIPE_KEY } from '$env/static/private';
```

Variables exposed to the browser must be prefixed `PUBLIC_`; everything else is private and importable only from server modules — `+page.server.ts`, `+server.ts`, and hooks — with the compiler rejecting violations at build time. The `static` variants are inlined when you build, which lets bundlers treat them as constants; the `$env/dynamic` variants read at runtime instead, for hosts where secrets rotate without a rebuild. The habit that keeps you honest: reach for `$env/static/private` first, because the import graph itself proves nothing leaked. This is the Chapter 1 promise applied to configuration — mistakes that would be production incidents become red squiggles.

## Testing: unit, component, end-to-end

Svelte projects test at three layers, and each has a standard tool. Unit tests cover pure logic with Vitest and need no Svelte at all:

```ts
// src/lib/pricing.test.ts
import { describe, expect, it } from 'vitest';
import { formatPrice } from './pricing';

describe('formatPrice', () => {
  it('renders minor units without drift', () => {
    expect(formatPrice(1299)).toBe('12.99');
  });
});
```

Component tests mount real components with `@testing-library/svelte` and assert on what a user would perceive:

```ts
// src/lib/Rating.test.ts
import { render, screen, fireEvent } from '@testing-library/svelte';
import { describe, expect, it, vi } from 'vitest';
import Rating from './Rating.svelte';

describe('Rating', () => {
  it('reports selection through its callback prop', async () => {
    const onSelect = vi.fn();
    render(Rating, { props: { value: 0, onSelect } });
    await fireEvent.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: '4 of 5' }));
    expect(onSelect).toHaveBeenCalledWith(4);
  });
});
```

Note what the test asserts: behavior through the component's public contract — the callback prop from Chapter 3, the accessible role — not its internals, which is what makes tests survive refactors. End-to-end tests drive a real browser with Playwright, exercising the full SvelteKit stack including server-rendered HTML:

```ts
// e2e/newsletter.spec.ts
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('newsletter form rejects bad input', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/newsletter');
  await page.fill('input[name=email]', 'not-an-email');
  await page.click('button:has-text("subscribe")');
  await expect(page.getByText('that is not an email')).toBeVisible();
});
```

The layering rule from the Rust chapter of your past applies verbatim: many fast unit and component tests, a few confident end-to-end tests on the critical paths — signup, checkout, the form above.

## Accessibility as a build step

Svelte's compiler treats accessibility as a build-time concern: it emits warnings for a catalog of defects — `a11y_missing_attribute` on unlabeled inputs, `a11y_click_events_have_key_events` on clickable divs, `a11y_invalid_attribute` on hrefless anchors — before your code ever reaches a browser. The culture that makes this valuable is refusing to normalize the warnings: a build with accessibility warnings is a failing build, the same standing you would give a type error. Silencing one with a comment is a decision that needs a written reason, because each silenced warning is a user who cannot use the feature.

The compiler catches markup; interaction quality is yours. Two habits cover most of the ground: manage focus deliberately — after opening a dialog, move focus into it and restore it on close; after route changes, land the user somewhere sensible rather than at the top of an unannounced page. For the announcement half, newer Svelte versions ship a `svelte/a11y` module with an `announce` helper that pipes messages into a polite live region for screen readers:

```svelte
<script>
  import { announce } from 'svelte/a11y';
  import { page } from '$app/state';

  $effect(() => {
    if (page.data?.title) announce(`loaded: ${page.data.title}`);
  });
</script>
```

Test with a keyboard only, at least once per feature: tab through, operate every control, watch where focus lands. The `{#if}` blocks and event attributes from earlier chapters already generate semantic HTML; the remaining discipline is behavioral, and it fits in a checklist.

## The launch checklist

Run this before every release, in order. Build with `npm run build` and read the output — compiler warnings first, then bundle sizes, since Chapter 1's model means your bundle is *exactly* what you wrote plus what you imported; code-split heavy routes with dynamic imports. Confirm the rendering strategy per route: prerendered where content is static, server-rendered where it is personal, `ssr = false` only with a reason written down. Verify environment boundaries: no private import reachable from client code, `PUBLIC_` keys actually public. Run the three test layers in CI, with the end-to-end suite covering the paths that make money. Clear every accessibility warning, and keyboard-test the newest feature. Check the 404 and error pages, because `+error.svelte` is part of the product. And if the destination is a desktop app, the same checklist applies with `adapter-static` feeding a Tauri shell — your Rust side and this side sharing one build pipeline. None of these steps is hard; the checklist exists because each one is easy to skip exactly once, and that once is the launch.

## Key takeaways

- The adapter is the only deploy-target decision — `adapter-auto` for platforms, `adapter-node` for servers and containers, `adapter-static` for CDNs — and changing it changes nothing else.
- Choose rendering per route: `prerender = true` for build-time HTML, SSR by default, `ssr = false` sparingly and with a reason.
- `$env/static/public` inlines safe values; `$env/static/private` is server-only and enforced at build time, making secret leaks compile errors instead of incidents.
- Test at three layers — Vitest units, `@testing-library/svelte` components through their public contract, Playwright flows through the real stack.
- Accessibility warnings are build output: treat them as failing CI, manage focus deliberately, and use `svelte/a11y`'s `announce` for screen-reader-visible state changes.

That closes the loop this book opened: a compiler that writes your plumbing, runes that make state legible, a framework that keeps the contract honest — and a launch where the remaining work is decisions, not heroics.

Everything so far assumed your components live in an app that already exists. SvelteKit is that app: the official framework around the compiler, owning the router, the server, the data-fetching contract, and the build targets. If Chapter 1's Svelte is the engine, SvelteKit is the chassis — and its design goal is the same one stated honestly: do at build time what can be done at build time, and keep a single contract that works with or without JavaScript.

## SvelteKit in one picture

A request hits SvelteKit and the machinery runs in a fixed order: the URL is matched against a route, the route's `load` functions fetch data (on the server, or in the browser for client-side navigation), the page component renders with that data, and the response is HTML on first visit and JSON thereafter. Forms can round-trip through *actions* without any JavaScript at all. You create a project with `npx sv create`, and the whole contract lives in a handful of special files, each named by role: `+page.svelte` renders, `+page.ts` or `+page.server.ts` loads, `+server.ts` answers HTTP, `+layout.svelte` wraps.

That naming is the trick worth internalizing — the `+` files are the framework's API surface. Everything else in `src/lib` is your ordinary code, imported wherever you like. The rest of this chapter walks the contract in the order a request meets it.

## File-based routing with +page and +layout

Routes are directories under `src/routes`; a directory becomes a URL segment, and a `+page.svelte` inside it becomes the page:

```text
src/routes/
├── +layout.svelte        ← shared shell: nav, footer, {#each}-free chrome
├── +page.svelte          ← /
├── about/
│   └── +page.svelte      ← /about
├── blog/
│   ├── +page.svelte      ← /blog
│   └── [slug]/
│       └── +page.svelte  ← /blog/:slug
└── (app)/                ← layout group: shared layout, no URL segment
    ├── dashboard/
    │   └── +page.svelte  ← /dashboard
    └── settings/
        └── +page.svelte  ← /settings
```

Square brackets make a dynamic parameter, reachable as `params.slug` in load functions and `$page`-free via props in the page. Parentheses make a *layout group* — several sections sharing one layout without the group name appearing in the URL, which is how you give marketing pages one shell and the app another. A `+layout.svelte` wraps every page below it and receives its children the way Chapter 3 taught: as a snippet, rendered with `{@render children()}`. Layouts nest, and each level's `+error.svelte` catches failures from below it. One rule to respect: only `+`-prefixed files participate in routing, so a stray `Button.svelte` dropped into a routes folder is inert.

## Load functions: universal versus server

Data arrives through `load` functions, and the choice between the two flavors is the first real decision on every route:

```ts
// src/routes/blog/[slug]/+page.server.ts — server only
import { error } from '@sveltejs/kit';
import { db } from '$lib/server/database';

export async function load({ params }) {
  const post = await db.post.findUnique({ where: { slug: params.slug } });
  if (!post) error(404, 'post not found');
  return { post };
}
```

```ts
// src/routes/blog/+page.ts — runs on server AND in the browser
import type { PageLoad } from './$types';

export const load: PageLoad = async ({ fetch, parent }) => {
  const { posts } = await parent();
  const comments = await fetch(`/api/comments?ids=${posts.map((p) => p.id)}`);
  return { comments: await comments.json() };
};
```

The split is about secrets and physics. `+page.server.ts` runs only on the server: it may use database clients, private environment variables (Chapter 9's `PRIVATE_` prefix), and anything else that must never reach the browser — and during client-side navigation it runs as a call to your own server. `+page.ts` is *universal*: it runs on the server for the first request and in the browser for subsequent navigation, which is what makes offline-friendly, serverless-style navigation possible. Its `fetch` is instrumented — results are recorded so the server-rendered pass can reuse them. Both flavors' returns are merged into one `data` object; `parent()` lets a child load await its layout's data. When a page only needs markup, it simply has no load file at all.

## Using load data

The page receives everything the loaders returned through the props mechanism from Chapter 3 — there is no global store to consult:

```svelte
<!-- src/routes/blog/[slug]/+page.svelte -->
<script lang="ts">
  import type { PageProps } from './$types';

  let { data }: PageProps = $props();
</script>

<svelte:head>
  <title>{data.post.title}</title>
</svelte:head>

<article>
  <h1>{data.post.title}</h1>
  <p>{data.post.body}</p>
</article>
```

`data` is a reactive `$props` entry, which matters more than it looks: when the route's data is invalidated — after a form action, after an explicit `invalidate` — the loaders re-run and `data` updates *in place*, and your template re-renders through the ordinary Chapter 2 machinery. The `PageProps` type comes from `./$types`, generated per-route by the compiler, so `data.post` is fully typed with no manual interface between the loader and the page. Keep the discipline of reading route data through `data` rather than importing shared state for page content; it is what lets SvelteKit prerender, refetch, and stream a page's data correctly.

## API routes with +server.ts

When a URL should answer with data instead of a page, `+server.ts` exports HTTP verb handlers:

```ts
// src/routes/api/subscribe/+server.ts
import { json } from '@sveltejs/kit';

export async function GET({ url }) {
  const email = url.searchParams.get('email');
  return json({ ok: Boolean(email) });
}

export async function POST({ request }) {
  const body = await request.formData();
  const email = String(body.get('email') ?? '');
  if (!email.includes('@')) {
    return json({ ok: false, problem: 'bad email' }, { status: 400 });
  }
  return json({ ok: true }, { status: 201 });
}
```

Each handler receives a standard `Request`-shaped event — `url`, `request`, `params`, `cookies`, `locals` — and returns a standard `Response`, with `json()` as the convenient wrapper. A `+server.ts` next to a `+page.svelte` serves that route's data directly, which is how you build endpoints mobile apps or scripts can call while the same route stays a page in the browser. This is also the natural home for webhooks and OAuth callbacks. One boundary to respect from Chapter 6: anything imported from `$lib/server/` is guaranteed server-only — the compiler turns an accidental client import into a build error rather than a leaked secret.

## Form actions and use:enhance

SvelteKit's most opinionated feature is that forms work without JavaScript, because the framework treats POST as a first-class route capability. A `+page.server.ts` can export `actions`:

```ts
// src/routes/newsletter/+page.server.ts
import { fail } from '@sveltejs/kit';

export const actions = {
  default: async ({ request }) => {
    const data = await request.formData();
    const email = String(data.get('email') ?? '');
    if (!email.includes('@')) {
      return fail(400, { email, problem: 'that is not an email' });
    }
    await subscribe(email);
    return { success: true };
  }
};
```

```svelte
<!-- src/routes/newsletter/+page.svelte -->
<script lang="ts">
  import { enhance } from '$app/forms';
  import type { PageProps } from './$types';

  let { form }: PageProps = $props();
</script>

<form method="POST" use:enhance>
  <input name="email" value={form?.email ?? ''} />
  {#if form?.problem}<p class="error">{form.problem}</p>{/if}
  {#if form?.success}<p>check your inbox</p>{/if}
  <button>subscribe</button>
</form>
```

The action runs on the server, and its return value arrives in the page's `form` prop — including `fail(400, …)`, which reports a failure without throwing. With no JavaScript, the browser POSTs, the server re-renders the page with `form` populated, and the user sees the result. With `use:enhance`, the same form is intercepted: the POST becomes a fetch, the action result is applied, and — crucially — the page's load functions and the `form` prop update reactively, exactly as if a full submission had happened. Named actions (`?/resubscribe`) let one page host several. This is progressive enhancement as architecture, not decoration: the server path is the real path, and `enhance` is an optimization layered on top.

## Invalidation, depends, and hooks

Because `data` is reactive, refresh becomes explicit and cheap. After a form action, SvelteKit automatically invalidates the affected load functions; anywhere else, you call `invalidate`:

```svelte
<script>
  import { invalidate } from '$app/navigation';

  async function refreshBalance() {
    await invalidate('app:balance');
  }
</script>
```

Loads declare interest with `depends('app:balance')`, a custom dependency string; `invalidate('app:balance')` re-runs every load that declared it, and `invalidateAll()` re-runs everything for the current route. The mechanism solves staleness without a global refetch — you name what changed, the framework finds who cares.

The last piece wraps every request: `hooks.server.ts` exports a `handle` function through which all traffic flows:

```ts
// src/hooks.server.ts
import type { Handle } from '@sveltejs/kit';

export const handle: Handle = async ({ event, resolve }) => {
  const token = event.cookies.get('session');
  event.locals.user = token ? await getUser(token) : null;
  return resolve(event);
};
```

`event.locals` is your per-request scratchpad — populated here, readable in every load, action, and `+server.ts` handler — and it is the correct home for request-scoped identity. Recall Chapter 6's warning about shared state in a server context: `locals` is the per-request replacement. Typed through `app.d.ts`, it closes the loop: the page gets its data from load, load gets its user from locals, and locals gets its user from the one place every request passes through.

## Key takeaways

- SvelteKit's contract is its `+` files: `+page.svelte` renders, `+page.ts` and `+page.server.ts` load, `+server.ts` answers HTTP, `+layout.svelte` wraps, `+error.svelte` catches.
- Server loads may touch databases and secrets; universal loads run on both sides and power client-side navigation — pick per route, not per project.
- Load data arrives as reactive `$props` typed by generated `PageProps`, and updates in place when invalidated.
- Form actions with `fail()` and the `form` prop make POST work without JavaScript; `use:enhance` layers the fetch-based version on top without changing the contract.
- `invalidate` and `depends` name what changed instead of refetching the world, and `hooks.server.ts` plus `locals` give every request a clean, per-user identity.

The app is built. What remains is shipping it — adapters, environments, tests, and accessibility — which is where the book goes next.

Most languages blur the line between "this is a bug" and "this is a thing that can go wrong." Rust refuses to. A broken invariant is a **panic** — loud, immediate, and honest. A missing file, a malformed input, a refused connection is a **`Result`** — a value like any other, present in your function's signature, impossible to forget to handle. This split is the whole philosophy: *recoverable failure is data, unrecoverable failure is an abort.*

That philosophy pays off in the place you spend your days reading code. When you call a function in Rust, its type tells you whether it can fail. `fn read_to_string(path) -> Result<String, io::Error>` cannot fail silently; `fn len(&self) -> usize` cannot fail at all. Error handling stops being archaeology of hidden exceptions and becomes ordinary, checkable plumbing.

## panic! — for bugs, not for weather

A panic unwinds the current thread, running `Drop` for every value on the way out (so resources still get released), and then terminates the thread — or the process, if it was the main thread. `panic!`, `assert!`, `unreachable!`, and slicing past the end of a vector all land here:

```rust
let v = vec![1, 2, 3];
// v[10];           // panics: index out of bounds — a real bug
```

The policy question is *when* to panic, and the ecosystem consensus is crisp: **panic when continuing would mean your program's assumptions are broken** — a configuration file that failed to parse at startup, an enum variant that cannot exist, an internal invariant violated by a bug in your own code. Tests are also a perfectly good place: an unexpected state in a test *should* panic.

And **libraries should almost never panic on inputs they cannot control.** If bad input is part of your function's operating environment — a user types a wrong path, a request contains malformed JSON — return `Err` and let the caller decide. A good heuristic: panic when the *program* is wrong; return `Err` when the *world* is wrong.

For the deliberate cases, `expect` and `unwrap` panic with context. The convention: `expect` with a message explaining the invariant, and reserve bare `unwrap` for prototypes and tests:

```rust
let port: u16 = std::env::var("PORT")
    .expect("PORT env var is set by our deployment harness");
```

## Result and Option

`Result<T, E>` is an enum with exactly two shapes:

```rust
enum Result<T, E> {
    Ok(T),
    Err(E),
}
```

Because it is an enum, the compiler forces you to consider both. The workhorse is `match`:

```rust
use std::fs;

fn read_config(path: &str) -> String {
    match fs::read_to_string(path) {
        Ok(text) => text,
        Err(err) => {
            eprintln!("warning: falling back to defaults: {err}");
            String::from("default=true")
        }
    }
}
```

`Option<T>` — `Some(T)` or `None` — is its sibling for "a value may legitimately be absent," with no error story attached. Mixing them up is a design smell: `None` means *there is no such thing*, `Err` means *we tried and something failed*. Converting between them is one call: `ok_or("reason")` turns an `Option` into a `Result`.

Both types come with a rich combinator vocabulary that keeps happy-path code flat — `map`, `and_then`, `unwrap_or`, `map_err` — worth knowing even though `?` will replace most of them:

```rust
let port = std::env::var("PORT")
    .ok()
    .and_then(|s| s.parse().ok())
    .unwrap_or(8080);
```

## The ? operator

Real code chains many fallible calls, and nesting `match` inside `match` does not scale. The `?` operator is the idiomatic answer: on `Ok`, unwrap and continue; on `Err`, **return early from the enclosing function**, converting the error type if needed:

```rust
use std::fs;
use std::num::ParseIntError;

fn read_port(path: &str) -> Result<u16, ParseIntError> {
    let text = fs::read_to_string(path)?;      // Err(io::Error) would need conversion...
    let port: u16 = text.trim().parse()?;      // ...so let's fix that below
    Ok(port)
}
```

The first `?` there is doing something subtle. `read_to_string` fails with `io::Error`, but the function returns `ParseIntError` — different types. The operator expands to roughly `match expr { Ok(v) => v, Err(e) => return Err(From::from(e)) }`, so it works whenever the function's error type implements `From` for the inner error. That single design choice — `?` delegates conversion to `From` — is what lets small error types compose into large ones without boilerplate at every call site. Fixing the function honestly:

```rust
use std::{fs, num::ParseIntError};

#[derive(Debug)]
enum ConfigError {
    Io(std::io::Error),
    Parse(ParseIntError),
}

impl From<std::io::Error> for ConfigError {
    fn from(e: std::io::Error) -> Self { ConfigError::Io(e) }
}

impl From<ParseIntError> for ConfigError {
    fn from(e: ParseIntError) -> Self { ConfigError::Parse(e) }
}

fn read_port(path: &str) -> Result<u16, ConfigError> {
    let text = fs::read_to_string(path)?;   // io::Error -> ConfigError::Io
    let port: u16 = text.trim().parse()?;   // ParseIntError -> ConfigError::Parse
    Ok(port)
}
```

Even `main` can wear a `Result`, which gives you fallible top-level setup with a clean message instead of a panic:

```rust
fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let port = read_port("config.txt")?;
    println!("serving on {port}");
    Ok(())
}
```

## Designing error types

The enum above is the seed of the pattern that scales: **one error enum per module or crate**, with a variant per failure mode, `Debug` and `Error` derived or implemented, and `From` impls so `?` folds lower-level errors in. Wrap foreign errors with context at the boundary where you understand *why* they happened:

```rust
#[derive(Debug)]
enum LoadError {
    MissingConfig { path: String },
    Io(std::io::Error),
}

impl std::fmt::Display for LoadError {
    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut std::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> std::fmt::Result {
        match self {
            LoadError::MissingConfig { path } =>
                write!(f, "config file not found: {path}"),
            LoadError::Io(e) => write!(f, "io failure while loading config: {e}"),
        }
    }
}

impl std::error::Error for LoadError {
    fn source(&self) -> Option<&(dyn std::error::Error + 'static)> {
        match self {
            LoadError::Io(e) => Some(e),
            _ => None,
        }
    }
}
```

Implementing `Error` and `source()` means your type composes with `Box<dyn Error>`, `anyhow`, and every logging/teardown tool in the ecosystem, which can walk the whole chain of causes.

In practice, two crates industrialize this. **`thiserror`** derives exactly the boilerplate above for library-shaped, typed errors:

```rust
use thiserror::Error;

#[derive(Debug, Error)]
pub enum LoadError {
    #[error("config file not found: {path}")]
    MissingConfig { path: String },
    #[error("io failure while loading config")]
    Io(#[from] std::io::Error),
}
```

**`anyhow`** is the application-side counterpart: `anyhow::Result<T>` is `Result<T, Box<dyn Error>>` with add-ons, and `.context("loading config")?` attaches human context to whatever bubbles up. The division of labor that has converged across the ecosystem: **typed error enums for libraries** (callers need to match on failure modes), **`anyhow` for binaries and applications** (the top level usually just reports and exits). Hand-roll the enums while learning — the derived crates make far more sense once you have written what they generate.

## Discipline and habits

A few policies that keep error handling sane as a codebase grows. Ban bare `.unwrap()` outside tests by habit (clippy's `unwrap_used` lint enforces it in CI). Prefer `expect("why this holds")` when panicking is genuinely correct — the message becomes part of the crash report. Never return `String` as an error type in a library: callers cannot match on it, and you lose the chain of causes. Add `#[non_exhaustive]` to public error enums so adding a variant later is not a breaking change for downstream matches. And when a failure mode is truly unrecoverable — poisoned mutexes, corrupted internal state — do not contort `Result` around it; panic, and document it.

The deepest habit is conceptual: errors flow *up* through `?` and are handled *once*, where someone can actually do something about them. No catching mid-stack "just in case," no swallowing. The type system keeps the channel honest; your job is to keep the messages useful.

## Key takeaways

- Split failure in two: panics for broken invariants and bugs, `Result` for failures the program should handle.
- `?` unwraps on success and returns early on error, converting via `From` — which is what lets error enums compose cleanly.
- Design one error enum per module with `From` impls from underlying errors; `thiserror` derives it for libraries, `anyhow` streamlines binaries.
- Libraries should avoid panicking on bad input; binaries may `expect` on setup invariants with a clear message.
- `Option` is absence, `Result` is failure — keep them distinct, convert deliberately, and let function signatures advertise fallibility.

Errors covered, the next hazard is time itself: many things happening at once. Chapter 5 brings the same compile-time rigor to threads, locks, and shared state.

Ownership told you *what* a value is; references told you *how long* you may use it. Traits answer the remaining design question: *what can you do with it?* A trait is a contract of behavior — methods that a type promises to provide — and generics are code written against that contract rather than against any concrete type. Together they replace the inheritance hierarchies of classical OOP with something stricter and faster: behavior checked at compile time, specialized per type, with zero runtime overhead unless you deliberately opt into it.

If you have used interfaces in Java or TypeScript, protocols in Swift, or typeclasses in Haskell, you have the right instincts. The Rust twist is that traits are resolved **statically by default** — and the consequences of that default run through everything in this chapter.

## Defining and implementing traits

A trait declares a set of methods. Some can be required (types must write them); some can be provided with default implementations:

```rust
trait Summary {
    fn author(&self) -> String;                 // required

    fn summarize(&self) -> String {             // provided default
        format!("Read more from {}...", self.author())
    }
}

struct Article {
    title: String,
    author: String,
}

struct Tweet {
    username: String,
    body: String,
}

impl Summary for Article {
    fn author(&self) -> String {
        self.author.clone()
    }
    fn summarize(&self) -> String {
        format!("{} — by {}", self.title, self.author)
    }
}

impl Summary for Tweet {
    fn author(&self) -> String {
        format!("@{}", self.username)
    } // summarize() comes from the default
}
```

Both types now satisfy the contract, each with its own behavior where it matters and shared behavior where it doesn't. Note the shape of the deal compared to inheritance: there is no base class, no constructor chaining, no subtype that secretly *is* another type. A struct is itself; a trait is something it can do. You may inherit behavior by composing traits, but you never inherit representation.

One rule to know early — the **orphan rule**: you may implement a trait for a type only if you own the trait or the type. Your `Summary` trait on your `Article`: fine. The standard library's `Display` on your `Article`: fine. `Display` on `Vec<i32>`: not allowed, because neither is yours. The rule exists to keep the ecosystem coherent — without it, two crates could each add conflicting behavior to the same type, and no `impl` anywhere would be trustworthy. When you hit it, the standard escape hatches are a **newtype wrapper** (`struct MyVec(Vec<i32>)`) or a local trait.

## Generic functions and trait bounds

Now write a function that works on anything summarizable. The explicit form spells out a **trait bound**:

```rust
fn notify<T: Summary>(item: &T) {
    println!("Breaking news: {}", item.summarize());
}
```

And the ergonomic shorthand — "any argument that implements `Summary`" — means exactly the same thing:

```rust
fn notify(item: &impl Summary) {
    println!("Breaking news: {}", item.summarize());
}
```

Here is the part that makes Rust generics genuinely different from Java-style interfaces. The compiler performs **monomorphization**: for every concrete type used with `notify`, it generates a specialized copy of the function, with the method call resolved to that type's implementation. `notify(&article)` becomes a call to one concrete function; `notify(&tweet)` becomes another. There is no interface table consulted at runtime, no virtual dispatch, no JIT warmup — the dispatch is as direct as if you had written both functions by hand. This is the "zero-cost" claim, and it is literal: **static dispatch to generics, with inlining available to the optimizer.**

Bounds compose, and `where` clauses keep complex signatures readable:

```rust
fn summarize_all<T>(items: &[T]) -> String
where
    T: Summary + Clone,
{
    items
        .iter()
        .map(|t| t.clone().summarize())
        .collect::<Vec<_>>()
        .join(" | ")
}
```

Returning `impl Trait` is the mirror image — a function that promises "some type implementing this trait," with the concrete type fixed and known to the compiler but hidden from callers:

```rust
fn best_article() -> impl Summary {
    Article {
        title: String::from("Zero-cost abstractions, for real"),
        author: String::from("H. Voss"),
    }
}
```

That is ideal for constructors and iterators you don't want to spell out. The one limitation: all return paths must produce the *same* concrete type. If you need to return different types from different branches, you want the next section.

## Trait objects and dynamic dispatch

Static dispatch has a cost of its own: specialization multiplies code and makes "a mixed bag of summarizable things" impossible to type, because a `Vec<T>` must hold exactly one `T`. When you need heterogeneity — or when binary size matters more than branch prediction — ask for dispatch through a **trait object**:

```rust
let feed: Vec<Box<dyn Summary>> = vec![
    Box::new(Article {
        title: String::from("Small stacks"),
        author: String::from("T. Klein"),
    }),
    Box::new(Tweet {
        username: String::from("hvoss"),
        body: String::from("traits are just types of types"),
    }),
];

for item in &feed {
    println!("{}", item.summarize()); // resolved at runtime via vtable
}
```

`dyn Summary` is an *unsized* type — the compiler cannot know how big each element is — so it lives behind a pointer (`&dyn Summary`, `Box<dyn Summary>`, `Rc<dyn Summary>`). At runtime each call goes through a **vtable**: one pointer indirection per call, plus the erasure of the concrete type. That is the entire cost, and it is the same deal you were implicitly making in any interface-heavy language.

The design habit worth building: **default to generics; reach for `dyn` at architectural seams.** A library's core algorithm wants monomorphization; a plugin registry, a heterogeneous collection, or a boundary where the set of types is genuinely open wants `dyn`. A trait is *object-safe* (dyn-compatible) when its methods don't return `Self` by value or use generics — the restriction exists precisely because a vtable caller must know the layout of what it gets back.

## The traits you will meet everywhere

A short tour of the standard library's load-bearing traits, because recognizing them is half of reading Rust fluently:

- **`Debug`** — formatting with `{:?}`. Derive it on everything you write; the panic and logging output of the entire ecosystem depends on it.
- **`Display`** — user-facing formatting with `{}`. Cannot be derived; you write it, and it is your type's public face.
- **`Clone` and `Copy`** — from chapter 1: explicit deep copy, and implicit bitwise copy respectively.
- **`PartialEq`/`Eq`** — `==`; **`PartialOrd`/`Ord`** — `<` and sorting; all derivable when the fields are.
- **`Default`** — a canonical empty value, derivable, and the backbone of the builder pattern.
- **`From`/`Into`** — infallible conversions. Implement `From<T> for U` and get `Into` for free; this is also what makes the `?` operator's automatic error conversion work in chapter 4.
- **`Iterator`** — the trait behind every `for` loop and the whole `map`/`filter`/`collect` ecosystem; chapter 9 leans on it hard.

Deriving the mechanical ones is the idiomatic starting point:

```rust
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Default)]
struct Config {
    name: String,
    retries: u32,
}
```

## Associated types, briefly

You will meet one more piece of syntax immediately in real code: traits with **associated types**, like `Iterator`:

```rust
pub trait Iterator {
    type Item;
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item>;
}
```

`type Item` says: each implementor picks one output type, once, for the whole trait. It keeps signatures clean (`Option<Self::Item>` instead of `Option<T>` with a `T` on every method) and encodes a real constraint — an iterator yields one kind of thing, not "any of several." When you design your own traits, the rule of thumb: use a generic parameter when a type may implement the trait many times with different types (`From<u8>` and `From<String>` both exist); use an associated type when there is exactly one natural choice per implementor.

## Key takeaways

- Traits are compile-time contracts of behavior; there is no inheritance of representation, only of interface, and the orphan rule keeps ecosystem impls coherent.
- Generics monomorphize: each concrete type gets its own specialized copy with direct calls — abstraction without runtime cost.
- `impl Trait` in argument position is shorthand for a generic bound; in return position it hides a single concrete type.
- `Box<dyn Trait>` enables heterogeneous collections and open extension via one vtable indirection — use it at architectural seams, not everywhere.
- The standard library's traits — `Debug`, `Clone`, `From`, `Iterator`, and friends — are the shared vocabulary of all Rust code; deriving them is the default starting move.

You can now describe behavior precisely. The next two chapters apply that machinery to the two things systems code cannot avoid: things go wrong (errors), and many things happen at once (concurrency).

Chapter 1 left you with an awkward reality: pass a `String` to a function and you lose it. Real programs need to *use* values without consuming them — read a config from three call sites, sort a vector in place, write a function that works on any slice it's handed. The mechanism for that is the **reference**, and the part of the compiler that checks references is the **borrow checker** — the component Rust's reputation is built on, for better and for worse.

This chapter gives you the actual rules, not folklore. They are shorter than you expect, and once you can state them precisely, most "why won't this compile" mysteries turn into straightforward arithmetic.

## References, and the two rules

A reference is a pointer with a compile-time contract. You create one with `&` (shared) or `&mut` (exclusive), and the contract is:

> At any given time, a value may have **any number of shared references** (`&T`), **or exactly one exclusive reference** (`&mut T`) — never both.

That's it. That is the whole borrow checker. Shared references are read-only views you can hand out freely. An exclusive reference is a promise, enforced by the compiler, that nothing else can read or write the value while it exists.

Why this rule and not something laxer? Two of the worst bugs in systems programming die exactly at this line. A **data race** requires two pointers to the same memory where at least one writes and neither is synchronized — the rule makes that combination unrepresentable. **Iterator invalidation** — mutating a collection while looping over it — is the same crime in miniature:

```rust
let mut v = vec![1, 2, 3];
for x in &v {
    // v.push(*x * 10);  // error: cannot borrow `v` as mutable
                          // while `v` is borrowed as immutable
    println!("{x}");
}
```

`push` may reallocate the buffer, which would leave the loop's reference pointing at freed memory. In C++ this is undefined behavior you discover in production; in Rust it is a compile error with a message that points at both parties.

The rule is symmetric and fair: hold as many readers as you like, or hold one writer, and the compiler arbitrates by *region* — a borrow lasts from where it is created to its last use, not merely to the end of the scope. This is **non-lexical lifetimes**, and it is why the following compiles:

```rust
let mut s = String::from("count");
let len = s.len();      // shared borrow starts...
println!("{len}");      // ...and ends here: last use of the borrow

s.push('!');            // fine: no shared borrow is alive anymore
```

## Borrowing in practice

Mutating through an exclusive reference requires the *owner* to be mutable, and there can be only one `&mut` at a time:

```rust
fn append_world(s: &mut String) {
    s.push_str(", world");
}

fn main() {
    let mut s = String::from("hello");
    append_world(&mut s);
    println!("{s}"); // hello, world
}
```

Notice what the signature `&mut String` communicates. The function borrows — it can mutate, but it does not own, so no cleanup happens when it returns, and the caller keeps its value. Compare that with `fn append_world(mut s: String)`, which would take ownership and destroy the caller's copy. Choosing between them is an API design decision you now make consciously, on every function.

Here is the classic triple-borrow puzzle, which reads like the rulebook narrating itself:

```rust
let mut scores = vec![8, 6, 9];

let first = &scores[0];        // shared borrow of scores
scores.push(10);               // error: needs exclusive borrow,
                               // but `first` is still alive
println!("{first}");
```

Reorder the lines so the shared borrow's last use comes before the mutation, and everything is fine:

```rust
let mut scores = vec![8, 6, 9];
let first = &scores[0];
println!("{first}");           // shared borrow ends here
scores.push(10);               // fine now
```

When the compiler *does* reject overlapping borrows inside one function, the fixes are almost always one of: reorder statements so borrows don't overlap, shrink the borrow's lifetime by introducing an inner block, restructure so you don't hold a reference across the mutation, or — when the pattern is genuinely two disjoint parts of one collection — split the borrow explicitly, which chapter 7 shows how to do for good reason.

## Slices: borrowing a view

A **slice** is a reference to a contiguous run of elements — a pointer plus a length. It is how Rust says "a borrowed view into some buffer," and it is everywhere once you learn to see it: `&str` is a slice of string bytes, `&[T]` is a slice of a `Vec` or array.

```rust
fn first_word(s: &str) -> &str {
    s.split_whitespace().next().unwrap_or("")
}

fn main() {
    let sentence = String::from("borrowing beats owning");
    let word = first_word(&sentence); // &String coerces to &str
    println!("{word}");
}
```

Two things are happening here. `&sentence` is a `&String`, but the function asks for `&str`, and Rust **coerces** automatically — deref coercion, one of the few invisible conversions in the language, and the reason APIs take `&str` rather than `&String`. The second thing: `word` borrows from `sentence`, so as long as `word` is alive, `sentence` cannot be mutated or dropped. The type system has turned "this string view depends on that string" into something the compiler tracks for you.

The same works for any collection:

```rust
let numbers = [10, 20, 30, 40];
let mid: &[i32] = &numbers[1..3]; // view of elements 1 and 2
assert_eq!(mid, &[20, 30]);
```

Slices are why Rust code takes `&[T]` instead of `&Vec<T>`: the slice accepts vectors, arrays, and sub-ranges alike, and the function never needs to know or care which.

## Lifetimes: the notation for "how long"

Every reference has a **lifetime** — the region of code during which the borrow is valid. Usually you never write it: the compiler infers it locally. You must write annotations only when references cross a function boundary in a way the compiler cannot guess, and the syntax is parameter-like:

```rust
fn longest<'a>(x: &'a str, y: &'a str) -> &'a str {
    if x.len() > y.len() { x } else { y }
}
```

Read `'a` not as a *thing* but as a **relationship**: "the returned reference is valid for exactly as long as *both* inputs are." The signature makes a promise the compiler can check at every call site — and, crucially, it makes the function honest about what it can return. Without the annotation, the compiler cannot know whether the result borrows from `x`, from `y`, or from neither, because that depends on a runtime branch.

A common first confusion: lifetimes do not *change* how long anything lives. They describe and enforce. The input values determine what is possible; the annotations let the compiler verify your function stays inside those bounds.

Structs that hold references must declare the same dependency:

```rust
struct Excerpt<'a> {
    part: &'a str,
}

let novel = String::from("call me ishmael. some years ago...");
let first = novel.split('.').next().unwrap();
let excerpt = Excerpt { part: first };
// `excerpt` cannot outlive `novel` — the compiler proves it
println!("{}", excerpt.part);
```

`Excerpt<'a>` means: an instance of this struct borrows from something, and while the instance exists, that something stays alive. This is why "self-referential structs" are famously hard in Rust — a struct holding a reference into itself would have to outlive itself, and the type system says no. The ecosystem answer is ownership-based (`String` fields instead of `&str`) or compile-time checked pointers like `ouroboros`/`self_cell` when you truly need it.

## Elision: why you rarely write lifetimes

If every function needed annotations, nobody would use the language. In practice, the compiler applies deterministic **elision rules** that write them for you: each reference parameter gets its own lifetime; if there is exactly one input lifetime, it is assigned to every output; and if a method takes `&self` or `&mut self`, its lifetime is assigned to all outputs. That is why these need no annotations:

```rust
fn first_word(s: &str) -> &str { /* ... */ }              // one input -> output borrows it
impl Excerpt<'_> {
    fn announce(&self) -> &str { self.part }              // &self -> output borrows self
}
```

You write `'a` when the rules would guess wrong — typically when there are two or more reference inputs and the output could borrow from either. The one lifetime you should recognize but rarely write is `'static`: it means "this reference is valid for the entire program run," which is true of string literals (they live in the binary itself) and of values deliberately leaked with `Box::leak`.

```rust
let literal: &'static str = "embedded in the binary";
```

## A mental model that survives contact

When the borrow checker rejects code, resist the urge to fight it blindly. Run this checklist instead:

- **Who owns the value, and where does it die?** Sketch the owner's scope; every reference must end before it.
- **Am I holding a borrow across an operation that needs exclusivity?** Shrink the borrow: move the use, add an inner scope, or clone at the boundary if the code is genuinely entangled.
- **Is the conflict inside one function, or across a signature?** Inside a function, reordering usually fixes it. Across signatures, the fix is structural — usually returning owned data or splitting the function.
- **Am I storing a reference where an owned value belongs?** Long-lived structs mostly want `String`, `Vec<T>`, and friends. Borrow fields are for views whose dependency you want checked.

The borrow checker is not asking you to prove cleverness. It is asking you to state, in the types, who depends on whom — and then it checks that statement everywhere, forever, for free. Chapter 3 builds on that foundation: traits and generics are how you describe shared *behavior* with the same compile-time rigor.

## Key takeaways

- The borrow checker enforces one rule: any number of shared borrows, or exactly one exclusive borrow, never both at once.
- Borrows end at their last use, not at the end of the scope — reorder statements and many "conflicts" dissolve.
- Slices (`&str`, `&[T]`) are borrowed views into buffers; APIs that take slices accept more callers and own nothing.
- Lifetime annotations describe relationships between references so the compiler can verify them; elision writes the common cases for you.
- Structs holding references declare their dependencies in the type — and the compiler proves the dependency is honored.

Chapter 5's threads are real OS entities: each costs about eight kilobytes of stack, a scheduling context, and a kernel transition whenever it blocks. A server holding a thread per connection dies around a few thousand sockets — not because the CPU is busy, but because it is *waiting* on I/O with nothing else to do. Async exists to fix exactly that: it lets one or a few threads juggle tens of thousands of tasks by making "waiting" an explicit, schedulable state instead of a blocked thread.

The mental correction to make first: **async is not about speed; it is about concurrency while waiting.** A CPU-bound program gains nothing from async — it may even lose. What async buys you is the ability to have enormous numbers of in-flight operations whose cost is a small state machine, not a thread.

## What a Future actually is

The core type is `Future`, and its definition is smaller than its reputation:

```rust
pub trait Future {
    type Output;
    fn poll(self: Pin<&mut Self>, cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<Self::Output>;
}

pub enum Poll<T> {
    Ready(T),
    Pending,
}
```

A future is a **lazy value describing work that may not be finished yet**. It does nothing when created. Someone repeatedly calls `poll`, and the future answers `Ready(value)` when done or `Pending` when not, registering a waker so the poller knows when to try again. When you write an `async fn`, the compiler rewrites it into an enum — one variant per `.await` point — that implements `Future`. Each `.await` becomes a state transition: save everything alive at that point, return `Pending`, and resume from exactly there when woken.

That is why "async fn is syntax sugar for a state machine" is the accurate slogan, and why the borrow checker from chapter 2 still governs everything: locals held across an `.await` live inside the future's enum, so they behave like fields of a struct being driven by an executor.

One crucial consequence of laziness: futures are inert until someone polls them. Unlike a JS promise, `let f = do_work();` runs *nothing*. Work starts when you `.await` it, spawn it, or hand it to a combinator.

## The executor: tokio

Who polls? That is the **executor** — the runtime, and the one part of this story Rust's standard library deliberately does not ship. In practice that means tokio: it owns a small pool of worker threads (typically one per core), multiplexes every spawned task across them, and integrates a timer and I/O driver so epoll/kqueue wakeups resume the right task.

```rust
use std::time::Duration;

async fn fetch(name: &str) -> String {
    tokio::time::sleep(Duration::from_millis(100)).await; // simulated I/O
    format!("{name} done")
}

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    // sequential: ~200ms total
    let a = fetch("users").await;
    let b = fetch("posts").await;

    // concurrent: ~100ms total — both futures driven together
    let (a, b) = tokio::join!(fetch("users"), fetch("posts"));

    println!("{a} · {b}");
}
```

Read `join!` carefully, because it is the shape of everyday async: both futures run *concurrently on one task*, interleaving at their await points — no spawning required. Spawning creates an independent task with its own lifetime:

```rust
#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    let handle = tokio::spawn(fetch("users")); // starts immediately

    println!("other work runs while the task is in flight");
    let result = handle.await.unwrap(); // JoinHandle, like thread::spawn
    println!("{result}");
}
```

The signature of `tokio::spawn` carries the contract — `F: Future + Send + 'static`. Two rules fall out that you will feel in real code: **what a spawned task owns must be owned** (or `Arc`-ed), because the task may outlive the function; and **it must be `Send`**, because the executor may migrate it between worker threads. A reference into your stack, or a non-`Send` type like `Rc`, will not compile into a spawned task — the same safety net as chapter 5, extended to state machines.

## Composition: racing, timing, selecting

The combinators are where async gets expressive. `tokio::join!` runs several operations to completion together. `tokio::select!` runs several and takes the **first** to finish, cancelling the rest — the primitive behind timeouts, heartbeats, and shutdown:

```rust
use tokio::time::{sleep, Duration};

async fn slow_query() -> String {
    sleep(Duration::from_secs(5)).await;
    String::from("results")
}

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    tokio::select! {
        res = slow_query() => println!("got: {res}"),
        _ = sleep(Duration::from_millis(300)) => {
            println!("timed out — query cancelled");
        }
    }
}
```

Cancellation deserves one careful sentence: when a `select!` branch loses, its future is simply dropped — and because `Drop` runs (chapter 1), cleanup is structural. But a drop can land *at any await point*, so code that must finish what it started (flushing a buffer, committing a transaction) should be written to tolerate interruption, or shielded with `tokio::spawn` so its lifetime is independent. Every async API you write is implicitly a cancellation surface; designing for that is part of the job, the same way designing for early return was in sync code.

A note on granularity: `await` points are where a task yields. Between them, a future is a synchronous block from the executor's point of view. Long stretches of CPU work between awaits stall the worker thread exactly like blocking I/O would — chunk your work so it awaits regularly.

## Blocking is the enemy

Every rule in async reduces to one commandment: **do not block the executor.** A worker thread stuck in a sync call — `std::thread::sleep`, `std::fs` file reads, a CPU-heavy loop, `reqwest::blocking` — is a thread that cannot poll any of its thousands of tasks. With one worker per core, a couple of careless blocking calls can stall an entire service.

The tools for the exceptions you cannot avoid:

```rust
// CPU-heavy or forced-sync work: move it off the async workers
let result = tokio::task::spawn_blocking(move || {
    expensive_hash(&big_buffer) // runs on a dedicated blocking pool
})
.await
.unwrap();
```

`spawn_blocking` hands the job to a separate pool sized for blocking work; `tokio::task::block_in_place` converts the *current* worker temporarily (multi-threaded runtime only). The selection rule is symmetric with chapter 5: **I/O-bound and many concurrent operations → async. CPU-bound → threads or spawn_blocking.** Mixing is fine and normal; hiding blocking inside async is not.

Two smaller traps worth naming. First, `std::sync::Mutex` across an `.await`: holding a guard across an await point both risks deadlock and (usually) makes your future non-`Send`. For short critical sections with no await inside, `std::sync::Mutex` is actually fine and fast; once you must hold a lock across awaits, switch to `tokio::sync::Mutex` deliberately. Second, recursion in async fns needs boxing (`Box::pin`) because the compiler must size the state machine — the `async_recursion` crate automates the workaround when you hit it.

## Async traits and streams

Two ecosystem notes to orient you. Since Rust 1.75, traits may declare `async fn` — but the built-in form has a wrinkle: it is not automatically object-safe (`dyn`) and the returned future is not guaranteed `Send`, which spawned tasks require. The mature ecosystem answers remain the **`async_trait`** macro (boxes the future for you, dyn-friendly) or returning `impl Future + Send` explicitly. When you see `#[async_trait]` in real codebases, that is what it is for.

When you need *many* values over time rather than one — a websocket, a watch stream, an event bus — the `Stream` trait is the async `Iterator`, and `while let Some(item) = stream.next().await` is its loop. Streams live in the `futures`/`tokio-stream` crates, not `std`, but they complete the picture: futures are one value later; streams are a sequence, item by await.

## Key takeaways

- Futures are lazy state machines: nothing runs until awaited, spawned, or combined; `.await` is a yield point the compiler compiles into state transitions.
- An executor (tokio) polls futures on a small thread pool sized to your cores — thousands of cheap tasks, each a enum, not a thread.
- `tokio::join!` runs futures concurrently on one task; `tokio::spawn` creates independent tasks that must be `Send + 'static` — ownership and thread-safety rules carry over intact.
- `select!` gives you first-wins racing and timeouts, with cancellation as structured drops — design for interruption at await points.
- Never block the executor: `spawn_blocking` for CPU work and forced-sync calls; `tokio::sync::Mutex` only when a lock genuinely spans an await.
- Async is for *waiting*, not for computation — choose it for I/O concurrency, and choose threads (chapter 5) for CPU parallelism.

You now have both halves of Rust's concurrency story. The next two chapters lift the safety net on purpose — first `unsafe`, the escape hatch and its contract, then FFI, where Rust meets the C world across the border.

Every operating system on earth speaks C. Drivers expose C headers, databases ship C clients, GPUs and codecs come as C libraries, and every language runtime — Python, Node, the JVM, the Web — defines its plugin boundary in C terms. Rust's answer to that world is FFI: a thin, explicit, *unsafe-at-the-border* layer that maps Rust types to C types and back. If chapter 7 taught you the contract of unsafe, this chapter is where you spend it — and where you learn to confine it to a thin strip of code called the boundary.

This matters doubly for you. Tauri — the framework in your own stack — is this pattern at scale: a Rust core, a webview front end, and a carefully designed boundary between them. FFI is the same idea one level down, minus the niceties.

## Calling C from Rust

An `extern` block declares foreign functions: names, signatures, and the ABI to call them with. Here is the classic first example — the C standard library, which on most platforms needs no explicit linking at all:

```rust
use std::os::raw::c_int;

extern "C" {
    fn abs(input: c_int) -> c_int;
    fn cos(input: f64) -> f64;
}

fn main() {
    let n: c_int = -7;
    // Safety: `abs` is a pure function on any c_int; no pointers involved.
    let result = unsafe { abs(n) };
    println!("|{n}| = {result}");
}
```

Everything about the `unsafe` is chapter 7 applied: the compiler *cannot* verify that `abs` exists, that its signature matches the real one, or that it behaves — you have transcribed a promise from a C header, and the unsafe block marks where that promise is spent. Get the signature wrong and there is no compile error at the call site; there is undefined behavior at runtime. Which is why real bindings are generated, not hand-written — that comes in a moment.

For libraries beyond libc, you declare the link target so the linker knows what to search for:

```rust
#[link(name = "sqlite3")]
extern "C" {
    fn sqlite3_libversion() -> *const std::os::raw::c_char;
}
```

## Mapping types across the border

The border needs type law, and the law is `#[repr(C)]`. By default, Rust orders struct fields however it likes; `repr(C)` freezes the layout into C-compatible order so both compilers agree byte-for-byte:

```rust
#[repr(C)]
pub struct Point {
    pub x: f64,
    pub y: f64,
}
```

Scalars map directly (`i32`/`u8`/`f64` are the same in both worlds); pointers map to `*const T`/`*mut T`; and the two Rust-only troublemakers need special handling. **Strings**: a C string is a null-terminated byte buffer; a Rust `String` is a pointer plus a length and *no terminator* — they are different animals. `CString`/`CStr` are the customs officers:

```rust
use std::ffi::{CStr, CString};
use std::os::raw::c_char;

fn strlen_demo(s: &str) {
    let c = CString::new(s).unwrap(); // copies + appends NUL
                                      // (fails if s contains an interior NUL)
    let p: *const c_char = c.as_ptr();
    unsafe {
        let len = libc::strlen(p);
        println!("len = {len}");
    }
}
```

Note the asymmetry, because it drives all the rules: *into* C you can copy (`CString::new`); *out of* C you must borrow cautiously (`CStr::from_ptr` trusts that the pointer is valid and NUL-terminated) and copy out (`to_string_lossy().into_owned()`) before anything else touches it. **Function pointers**: C allows null function pointers, and Rust's `fn` type does not — the idiom is `Option<unsafe extern "C" fn(u32)>`, where `None` is the null case. Rust guarantees the niche optimization makes it the same size.

## Exporting Rust: being the library

The other direction makes Rust functions callable from C — the building block for plugins in any language, shared libraries, and Tauri-style cores. Two annotations do the work: `#[no_mangle]` keeps the symbol name un-mangled so C can find it, and `extern "C"` picks the calling convention:

```rust
use std::os::raw::c_char;

#[no_mangle]
pub extern "C" fn rust_add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {
    a + b
}
```

`rust_add` now appears in the shared library as a plain C symbol — callable from Python's `ctypes`, Node's FFI, C, Swift, whatever. Compile with `crate-type = ["cdylib"]` in `Cargo.toml` for a C-compatible dynamic library, or `staticlib` for static linking.

Numbers are trivial. The moment ownership crosses the border, you are designing an API contract. The pattern for heap objects: hand out an opaque pointer, and pair every constructor with an explicit destructor that C must call:

```rust
/// Opaque handle: C sees a pointer, never the struct.
#[repr(C)]
pub struct Buffer {
    data: Vec<u8>,
}

#[no_mangle]
pub extern "C" fn buffer_new(len: usize) -> *mut Buffer {
    Box::into_raw(Box::new(Buffer { data: vec![0; len] }))
}

/// # Safety
/// - `buf` must come from `buffer_new` and not be used after this call.
#[no_mangle]
pub unsafe extern "C" fn buffer_free(buf: *mut Buffer) {
    if !buf.is_null() {
        drop(Box::from_raw(buf)); // ownership returns to Rust: Vec is dropped
    }
}

/// # Safety
/// - `buf` must be valid, and `out` must have room for `len` bytes.
#[no_mangle]
pub unsafe extern "C" fn buffer_read(buf: *const Buffer, out: *mut u8, len: usize) {
    let buf = &*buf; // borrow the Box'd value
    let out = std::slice::from_raw_parts_mut(out, len);
    let n = len.min(buf.data.len());
    out[..n].copy_from_slice(&buf.data[..n]);
}
```

Study the shape: `Box::into_raw` *transfers* ownership across the border (chapter 1's move, with C as the new owner); `Box::from_raw` takes it back, and the `Drop` runs on the Rust side where it belongs. The `# Safety` sections are the API's terms of service — C cannot break the borrow checker, so the doc comments and disciplined exports are the enforcement.

**Panics must not unwind across the border** — C has no idea what a Rust panic is. Either compile with `panic = "abort"`, or guard each exported function:

```rust
#[no_mangle]
pub extern "C" fn safe_entry(x: i32) -> i32 {
    match std::panic::catch_unwind(|| might_panic(x)) {
        Ok(v) => v,
        Err(_) => -1, // C-visible error code instead of UB
    }
}
```

## Bindings at scale

Hand-writing signatures for a real C library does not scale, and worse — typos become silent UB. The ecosystem's answer is generation in both directions. **bindgen** reads C headers and emits the `extern` blocks (compiled against `libclang`); **cbindgen** reads your Rust and emits C headers for your exported functions. The convention that ties crates.io together is the **`-sys` crate**: `foo-sys` contains *only* generated declarations and linking logic, raw and unsafe; a safe `foo` crate (often the same project) wraps it with real Rust types, `Result`s, and lifetimes — chapter 7's wall, drawn around every C dependency in the ecosystem.

```toml
# Cargo.toml
[dependencies]
rusqlite = { version = "0.32", features = ["bundled"] }
```

`rusqlite` is the pattern executed end-to-end: it vendors and compiles SQLite (via `libsqlite3-sys`, bindgen-generated), then hands you `Connection` and `Statement` types with RAII closing, `Result` everywhere, and zero unsafe in your code. When you must bind something yourself, copy that layering: `-sys` raw, wrapper safe, boundary thin.

One more border rule worth engraving: **thread safety is part of the contract.** If the C library says "not thread-safe," your wrapper must prove it — wrap the handle in a type that is not `Send`/`Sync` (a raw pointer is neither, by default, which is the safe starting point) or serialize access behind a mutex, and only `unsafe impl Send` (chapter 7) when the C documentation genuinely warrants it.

## The border in your stack

The pattern generalizes beyond C. **PyO3** exposes Rust as Python modules with GIL-aware wrappers; **napi-rs** does the same for Node; **wasm-bindgen** for the browser; **UniFFI** generates bindings for Kotlin/Swift from one Rust core. Tauri sits in this family too — its *command* system is an ergonomic boundary layer (JSON-typed, promise-based) over the same Rust-core/foreign-front-end divide, and the same design instincts apply: validate at the border, own your side's memory, keep the unsafe — or the serialization — confined to one thin layer, and everything behind it stays idiomatic Rust.

## Key takeaways

- `extern` blocks declare C functions; the call is `unsafe` because *you* vouch that the signature and behavior match the real library.
- `#[repr(C)]` fixes struct layout; `CString`/`CStr` translate strings; `Option<extern "C" fn>` represents nullable callbacks.
- Export with `#[no_mangle] pub extern "C" fn`, hand out opaque `Box::into_raw` handles, pair constructors with `_free` destructors, and never let a panic unwind across the border.
- Real bindings are generated — bindgen for C-to-Rust, cbindgen for Rust-to-C — and layered as raw `-sys` crates plus safe wrappers.
- Thread-safety claims about foreign libraries are contracts: default handles to non-`Send`, and only `unsafe impl` with documented evidence.

You now hold the full language: memory, behavior, errors, both concurrency models, and the border. One thing remains — turning all of it into something a user can install and run. The final chapter ships a real CLI, from `cargo new` to crates.io.

Everything so far has been the compiler checking your work. `unsafe` is where the direction reverses: you take the checker's seat, with a specific and demanding job description. The misconception to kill immediately — `unsafe` is **not** "Rust with the borrow checker disabled." The borrow checker still runs inside unsafe blocks; ownership, borrows, and lifetimes still apply. What unsafe actually grants is the ability to perform operations the compiler *cannot verify*: raw pointer dereferences, calls to foreign functions, mutation of statics. For those, no automatic check exists — so the obligation shifts from compiler to author.

The number to keep in mind: real Rust codebases typically hold unsafe to a few percent, concentrated in foundational layers. The standard library's `split_at_mut`, `Vec`, `String`, `Arc` — all use unsafe internally so that *your* code never has to. The discipline of the language is precisely that: **unsafe is a wrapper's tool, and the wrapper's job is to make it invisible.**

## The five superpowers

Writing `unsafe` does five things, no more. Inside an `unsafe` block (or `unsafe fn`), you may:

1. **Dereference raw pointers** (`*const T`, `*mut T`);
2. **Call `unsafe` functions**, including all FFI (chapter 8);
3. **Access or modify `static mut` variables**;
4. **Read fields of `union`s**;
5. **Implement `unsafe` traits**, chiefly `Send` and `Sync`.

Everything else — moves, borrows, drops, even allocation — stays in the safe world. Here is the canonical raw-pointer tour:

```rust
let mut num = 5;

let r1 = &num as *const i32;   // creating raw pointers is SAFE
let r2 = &mut num as *mut i32; // and so is this

unsafe {
    println!("r1: {}", *r1); // dereferencing is the unsafe act
    *r2 = 10;                // so is writing through one
}
println!("{num}");
```

Note the split: *creating* a raw pointer (aliasing an address) is safe and can be done in safe code; *dereferencing* asserts "I guarantee this pointer is valid, aligned, initialized, and — for writes — not aliased," and that assertion is what requires `unsafe`. Rust 2024 also gives you the cleaner `&raw const num` / `&raw mut num` syntax for creating them.

Why do raw pointers exist at all, when references exist? Three honest reasons: they can alias freely (multiple raw pointers to the same data is legal, unlike `&mut`); they have no lifetime attached (they can be stored in structs and handed across APIs where a reference would embed a lifetime); and they are the currency of FFI — C's `T*` maps to `*mut T`, full stop. They are a *worse* reference and a *universal* interface at the same time.

## The actual contract

The unsafe block is a promise with named clauses. When you dereference `*mut T` or call an unsafe function, you are asserting:

- **Valid memory** — the pointer points into an allocated, properly aligned, initialized object that is still alive.
- **Exclusive access for writes** — no other code reads or writes that memory while the write happens (the aliasing rule from chapter 2, now enforced by *you*).
- **Valid values** — for types like `bool`, `&T`, `char`, or enums, the bits you produce must be a legal value; `mem::transmute` to a type whose bit patterns don't match is instant undefined behavior.
- **No unwinding across borders** — panicking out through FFI (chapter 8) or through certain `extern` calls is UB; catch it with `catch_unwind` where it can happen.

Break any clause and you get **undefined behavior** — not a crash, but a license for the optimizer to assume something impossible, which manifests as corruption, "impossible" miscompiles, or security holes that appear only under optimization. This is why unsafe review comments read like legal contracts, and why the community norm is explicit:

```rust
/// # Safety
///
/// - `ptr` must be valid for writes of `len` elements of `T`.
/// - `ptr` must be non-null and correctly aligned.
/// - Caller must ensure no other access to the region during the call.
pub unsafe fn fill(ptr: *mut u8, len: usize, value: u8) { /* ... */ }
```

If an `unsafe fn` or block cannot state its invariants in a doc comment, it is not ready to ship.

## Case study: wrapping unsafe in a safe API

Here is the pattern that justifies the whole chapter — borrow-checked code that the borrow checker cannot prove, made safe by hand. The task: split a mutable slice into two non-overlapping halves. Straight references are illegal (two `&mut` to the same slice), but the operation is obviously sound if `mid` is in bounds — the regions genuinely do not overlap. The standard library's `split_at_mut` does exactly this dance:

```rust
fn split_at_mut(values: &mut [i32], mid: usize) -> (&mut [i32], &mut [i32]) {
    let len = values.len();
    let ptr = values.as_mut_ptr();

    assert!(mid <= len); // ← the invariant, checked HERE, once

    unsafe {
        (
            std::slice::from_raw_parts_mut(ptr, mid),
            std::slice::from_raw_parts_mut(ptr.add(mid), len - mid),
        )
    }
}

fn main() {
    let mut v = [10, 20, 30, 40];
    let (left, right) = split_at_mut(&mut v, 2);
    left[0] += 1;
    right[0] += 1;
    println!("{v:?}"); // [11, 20, 31, 40]
}
```

Read the structure, because it is *the* template: an `assert!` turns an uncheckable promise into a checked precondition; the unsafe block is three lines long; and the function signature is **100% safe** — callers cannot misuse it, no matter what they do. All of the risk was purchased by the author and paid once, inside a walled garden. `from_raw_parts_mut` is itself `unsafe` (with the same contract: valid, aligned, initialized, exclusive), and our assert is what lets us honestly claim it.

This is why "how much unsafe is in a project" is the wrong question. The right questions: is it in a leaf layer? Is every block justified by a written invariant? Does it leak into public safe signatures?

## unsafe impl Send/Sync and the rest of the list

The fifth superpower deserves its own warning, because it has no code block to point at. `unsafe impl Send for MyType` asserts "my type really can move between threads safely" — an assertion about *your* aliasing design that the compiler cannot check. You reach for it when wrapping a non-`Send` foreign type that you know is thread-safe (a C library handle documented as thread-safe, say), and the doc comment must cite the evidence. The same story covers `static mut` — a global mutable variable is an instant data race unless access is synchronized, which is why modern style prefers `static` + `Mutex`/atomics (chapter 5) — and `union` fields, where reading past the last-written variant is your responsibility (Rust unions are FFI/interop tools, not C-style convenience).

Since Rust 2024, unsafe blocks must carry an `unsafe(...)` attribute listing the operations they rely on (`unsafe(op_unsafe_access)`), and unused unsafe blocks are linted — the language is actively narrowing the blast radius.

## Tools and discipline

You are fallible, so mechanize the checking. **Miri** — `cargo +nightly miri test` — is an interpreter that detects undefined behavior in unsafe code: invalid frees, out-of-bounds access, aliasing violations, uninitialized reads. It is slow, and it is non-negotiable for unsafe-heavy crates. **Clippy** catches unsafe antipatterns; **fuzzing** (cargo-fuzz) and **AddressSanitizer** builds stress the boundaries. Discipline wraps the tools:

- **Minimize the surface**: unsafe lives in dedicated modules with `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]` everywhere else.
- **Wrap, don't expose**: public APIs are safe; unsafe is an implementation detail behind checked invariants.
- **Write the contract**: every unsafe block gets a comment naming the exact invariants it assumes and where they are enforced.
- **Prefer safe primitives first**: `Arc<Mutex>`, atomics, `mem::take`, `itertools` — most "I need unsafe" impulses dissolve against them.

And keep perspective: you already *depend* on unsafe constantly. `Vec`, `Box`, `String`, `Mutex`, `tokio`'s scheduler, the OS interface — all contain unsafe, audited by thousands of eyes. Learning to read and write it makes you a complete Rust programmer, not a daredevil.

## Key takeaways

- unsafe grants exactly five abilities — raw deref, unsafe calls, `static mut`, union fields, unsafe trait impls — and the borrow checker keeps running inside the block.
- The unsafe author owns memory-safety invariants: validity, alignment, initialization, and write-exclusivity; breaking them is undefined behavior, not a panic.
- The template is `assert!` + tiny unsafe block + safe public signature: check what can be checked, isolate what cannot, never leak the unsafety.
- `unsafe impl Send/Sync` is a claim about your type's aliasing design — make it only with documented evidence.
- Use Miri, clippy, and fuzzing to check the checker; confine unsafe with `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]` and keep public APIs safe.

The largest consumer of unsafe in practice is the border with C. The next chapter walks straight up to that border: FFI, where Rust's invariants meet code that has never heard of them.

There are two broad cultures of concurrent programming, and Rust speaks both fluently. The first inherits from C and POSIX: you spawn operating-system threads, share memory, and coordinate access with locks. The second inherits from Erlang and Go: you create workers and pass messages between them, so nothing mutable is ever shared. Most languages pick a favorite. Rust does something better than picking: it makes *both* safe, by extending the ownership rules from chapter 1 across thread boundaries.

The headline is easy to state: **a data race is a compile error in Rust.** Not a lint, not a sanitizer that runs in CI — a compile error, produced by two marker traits (`Send` and `Sync`) that describe how values may cross thread boundaries. This chapter builds up from plain threads to the full toolbox, and ends with the rules of thumb that make the choices easy.

## Threads and ownership

`std::thread::spawn` starts an OS thread and returns a `JoinHandle` — drop it to let the thread run unjoined, or call `.join()` to wait for it and collect its result:

```rust
use std::thread;

fn main() {
    let data = vec![1, 2, 3];

    let handle = thread::spawn(move || {
        println!("worker got: {data:?}");
    });

    println!("main continues while worker runs");
    handle.join().unwrap();
}
```

That `move` keyword is doing quiet, important work. A spawned thread may outlive the function that created it, so the compiler refuses to let the closure *borrow* stack-local data — the borrow could dangle. `move` transfers ownership of `data` into the closure, and now the compiler can prove the worker owns everything it touches. This is ownership doing concurrency bookkeeping: **the value moved, therefore nobody else can touch it, therefore the thread is safe.**

Join handles compose with `Result` naturally — `join()` returns `Result<T, E>` where `E` is whatever the thread panicked with, so a worker panic can propagate instead of being swallowed.

## Shared state: Arc and Mutex

Owning everything in one thread gets you only so far. When state genuinely must be shared, Rust's rule from chapter 2 applies unchanged: **mutation requires exclusivity.** Across threads, that means two wrappers in partnership.

`Mutex<T>` is the lock — but unlike a C mutex, it *owns* the data it guards. You cannot touch the contents without locking, and the lock guard returned by `.lock()` is an RAII type from chapter 1: it releases the mutex when it goes out of scope. Forgetting to unlock is structurally impossible.

`Arc<T>` — atomic reference counting — solves the second half of the problem: multiple threads each owning a *handle* to the same shared value. It is `Rc` with atomic ops, and the cost is an increment/decrement per clone.

```rust
use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};
use std::thread;

fn main() {
    let counter = Arc::new(Mutex::new(0));
    let mut handles = Vec::new();

    for _ in 0..10 {
        let counter = Arc::clone(&counter);
        handles.push(thread::spawn(move || {
            let mut count = counter.lock().unwrap();
            *count += 1;
        })); // guard dropped here: lock released
    }

    for h in handles {
        h.join().unwrap();
    }

    println!("result: {}", *counter.lock().unwrap()); // 10, every run
}
```

Now watch what the type system does with the famous mistakes. Replace `Mutex` with `RefCell` and the program will not compile — `RefCell` is not `Sync`, so `Arc<RefCell<T>>` cannot cross threads; the counter-increment would be a data race and the compiler stops it. Drop the `Arc` and it will not compile either — the closure would try to move `counter` into every thread at once, and ownership says no. What the compiler *cannot* do is prevent deadlocks: lock two mutexes in opposite orders in two threads, and you have a logic bug no type system catches. Two habits prevent nearly all deadlocks in practice: **always acquire multiple locks in the same global order**, and keep critical sections as small as possible.

One more detail that surprises people: when a thread panics while holding a mutex, the mutex becomes *poisoned*, and every later `.lock()` returns `Err`. Poisoning is a feature — it propagates "something went catastrophically wrong here" rather than letting other threads continue against potentially inconsistent state. `lock().unwrap()` is common precisely because most programs treat a poisoned lock as fatal; use `.unwrap_or_else(|e| e.into_inner())` when you genuinely want to recover.

## Channels: share by communicating

The message-passing culture avoids shared mutable state entirely: workers own their data, and everything else arrives as messages. The standard library ships a multi-producer, single-consumer channel:

```rust
use std::sync::mpsc;
use std::thread;

fn main() {
    let (tx, rx) = mpsc::channel();

    for id in 0..3 {
        let tx = tx.clone();
        thread::spawn(move || {
            let msg = format!("hello from worker {id}");
            tx.send(msg).unwrap(); // blocking send for unbounded channels
        });
    }

    drop(tx); // release the original sender: rx can drain and finish

    for msg in rx { // rx is an iterator that blocks until closed
        println!("{msg}");
    }
}
```

The type signature tells the safety story again: `Sender<T>` is `Send` and owns its end; `send` *moves* the message into the channel, so the sending thread cannot keep using it. Ownership is transferred across the channel, statically checked. If you have Go experience: there is no `select` in `std`'s channel (chapter 6 shows async `select!`), and channels are not magic — they are a queue plus locking under the hood.

The design instinct: **channels when work decomposes into independent jobs** (parsers, request handlers, pipeline stages); **`Arc<Mutex<T>>` when a small piece of state is genuinely shared** (a counter, a config, a connection registry). Both patterns coexist happily in large systems.

## Scoped threads: borrowing without Arc

`Arc` is the price of threads that might outlive their inputs — but many threads are short-lived and clearly joined before the data goes away. Since Rust 1.63, `thread::scope` lets workers *borrow* from the enclosing scope, because the API guarantees all threads are joined before the borrow ends:

```rust
use std::thread;

fn main() {
    let nums = vec![1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8];

    let total: i64 = thread::scope(|s| {
        let handles: Vec<_> = nums
            .chunks(2)
            .map(|chunk| s.spawn(move || chunk.iter().sum::<i64>()))
            .collect();

        handles.into_iter().map(|h| h.join().unwrap()).sum()
    });

    println!("total: {total}");
}
```

No clone, no `Arc`, no channel — each worker borrows its `&[i64]` chunk directly, and the compiler verifies the borrows end within the scope. For "split an array, process, join" workloads this is the modern default, and it deletes a whole category of refcount noise.

## Send and Sync: the two traits behind it all

Everything above rests on two auto traits you rarely name but rely on constantly:

- **`Send`** — a type may be *transferred* to another thread. Most types are; `Rc<T>` is famously not, because its refcount is not atomic, which is exactly why `Rc` cannot leak into thread code.
- **`Sync`** — a type may be *referenced* from multiple threads: `T` is `Sync` if `&T` is `Send`. `Mutex<T>` is `Sync` (that is its job); `RefCell<T>` is not (its borrow checking is single-threaded); raw pointers are neither.

These traits compose automatically through the types you build: a struct is `Send`/`Sync` exactly when its fields are. And they plug into the ownership system at the points of transfer — `thread::spawn` requires its closure to be `Send + 'static`, channel sends require `Send`, shared references across threads require `Sync`. When the compiler says "cannot be sent between threads safely," it is telling you that some field — often an `Rc`, a `RefCell`, or a raw pointer — cannot uphold the sharing rules. The fix is usually to swap that field for its thread-safe twin (`Arc`, `Mutex`, atomics).

## Rayon and the rules of thumb

For pure data-parallel work — transform or fold a big collection — you rarely need to touch threads at all. **Rayon** converts an iterator to a parallel one and handles partitioning, work-stealing, and joining:

```rust
use rayon::prelude::*;

fn sum_of_squares(n: u64) -> u64 {
    (0..n).into_par_iter().map(|x| x * x).sum()
}
```

Because Rayon requires the closure to be `Send` and the items to be `Sync`, it inherits all of the safety machinery above — parallelism with the same compiler guarantees. There is no refactor risk: `par_iter()` is a drop-in for a surprising fraction of iterator code.

After all the tools, the decision procedure fits on an index card — keep it in your notebook:

- **Independent, CPU-bound work over a collection?** Rayon (`par_iter`), or scoped threads for hand-partitioned chunks.
- **Independent, I/O-bound work at scale (thousands of tasks)?** Async — chapter 6.
- **A few long-running OS jobs?** Plain `thread::spawn` with `move`.
- **Sharing a small piece of state?** `Arc<Mutex<T>>`; keep the critical section tiny.
- **Sharing a lot of state?** Redesign until you aren't — channels, partitioning, or an owned-per-worker architecture.

That last line is the deepest one. The borrow checker will not let you write a data race, but it will happily compile a correct program that spends half its runtime acquiring locks it did not need. Concurrency is not a seasoning you add; it is a structure you choose — and the smallest structure that works is almost always the fastest one.

## Key takeaways

- `thread::spawn` + `move` transfers ownership into workers; `join()` collects results and panics propagate through it.
- `Mutex<T>` owns its data and unlocks via RAII guards; `Arc<T>` shares the handle — together they make shared mutable state safe, and the classic mistakes fail to compile.
- Channels (`std::sync::mpsc`) move ownership between threads as messages — the right default when work decomposes into independent jobs.
- `thread::scope` (1.63+) lets workers borrow directly, deleting `Arc` noise for short-lived parallel sections.
- `Send`/`Sync` are the compiler's proof vocabulary for thread safety: `Rc`/`RefCell` are excluded, `Arc`/`Mutex` are included, and your structs inherit the verdicts automatically.
- Deadlocks are still your problem: consistent lock order and small critical sections are the discipline.

Threads are the heavyweight culture — eight kilobytes of stack and real syscalls. The next chapter is the other culture: async, where thousands of tasks fit where one thread used to, and the trade-offs move from the OS to the runtime.

Everything before this chapter was the language. This chapter is the *practice* — the last mile between a working function and something a user installs with `cargo install` and runs in a terminal. We will build `findr`, a small grep-like tool, not because the world needs another grep, but because a CLI exercises every layer you have learned: ownership at the API boundary, `Result` discipline, iterators, `Send`/`Sync` in testing, and Cargo's release machinery. The structure you build here — thin binary, testable core — is the same shape you will use for services and Tauri backends.

## Project layout: thin binary, testable core

`cargo new findr` gives you a `main.rs` — and the single most valuable structural decision you can make on day one is to move the logic *out* of it:

```text
findr/
├── Cargo.toml
└── src/
    ├── lib.rs      ← the engine: pure, testable, reusable
    ├── main.rs     ← the shell: args, I/O, exit codes
    └── bin/        ← (optional) extra binaries sharing lib.rs
```

The rule of thumb: **`main.rs` parses input and reports output; `lib.rs` does the work.** Library code can be unit-tested without spawning a process, reused by other binaries (or by your Tauri app's core), and evolves independently of the CLI skin. A `main.rs` you can print on one screen is a quiet signal of a healthy project.

## Parsing arguments with clap

Arguments arrive raw in `std::env::args()` — strings, in order, no help text, no validation. For anything beyond one flag, use **clap**, the ecosystem standard, whose derive API turns a struct into a full-featured CLI:

```toml
[dependencies]
clap = { version = "4", features = ["derive"] }
```

```rust
use clap::Parser;
use std::path::PathBuf;

/// Search for a pattern in a file and display the lines that match.
#[derive(Parser, Debug)]
#[command(name = "findr", version, about)]
struct Args {
    /// The pattern to search for
    pattern: String,

    /// The path of the file to read
    path: PathBuf,

    /// Case-insensitive matching
    #[arg(short, long)]
    ignore_case: bool,
}

fn main() {
    let args = Args::parse(); // exits with usage message if invalid
    println!("searching for '{}' in {}", args.pattern, args.path.display());
}
```

That derive alone buys `--help` and `--version` with formatted output, type-validated positional args, flags, and error messages users can act on. The doc comments become the help text — documentation and UX from one source. Notice the types doing chapter 2's work: `PathBuf` rather than `String` for the path means invalid-path handling is a type-level concern, not a runtime convention.

## The core: find_matches in lib.rs

The engine is a pure function — no I/O, no global state, trivially testable:

```rust
// src/lib.rs
pub fn find_matches<'a>(content: &'a str, pattern: &str, ignore_case: bool) -> Vec<&'a str> {
    if ignore_case {
        let lowered = pattern.to_lowercase();
        content
            .lines()
            .filter(|line| line.to_lowercase().contains(&lowered))
            .collect()
    } else {
        content
            .lines()
            .filter(|line| line.contains(pattern))
            .collect()
    }
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    #[test]
    fn finds_matching_lines() {
        let text = "alpha\nbeta\nalphabet";
        assert_eq!(find_matches(text, "alpha", false), vec!["alpha", "alphabet"]);
    }

    #[test]
    fn ignores_case_when_asked() {
        let text = "Alpha\nbeta";
        assert_eq!(find_matches(text, "alpha", true), vec!["Alpha"]);
    }

    #[test]
    fn no_matches_is_empty() {
        assert!(find_matches("one\ntwo", "zzz", false).is_empty());
    }
}
```

The lifetimes in the signature are chapter 2 speaking plainly: the returned lines borrow from `content` — no copies, and the compiler enforces that the caller keeps the buffer alive. `cargo test` runs this suite in milliseconds, no process spawning, no filesystem. A real tool would return an iterator instead of a `Vec` (chapter 3's `Iterator` trait) to stream lazily; the `Vec` keeps the example legible.

## main.rs: errors, exit codes, and the two streams

Now the shell, with the error policy from chapter 4 executed precisely — read the file, fail with a message on **stderr**, exit non-zero:

```rust
use clap::Parser;
use std::path::PathBuf;
use std::process::ExitCode;

use findr::find_matches;

/// Search for a pattern in a file and display the lines that match.
#[derive(Parser, Debug)]
#[command(name = "findr", version, about)]
struct Args {
    /// The pattern to search for
    pattern: String,

    /// The path of the file to read
    path: PathBuf,

    /// Case-insensitive matching
    #[arg(short, long)]
    ignore_case: bool,
}

fn main() -> ExitCode {
    let args = Args::parse();

    let content = match std::fs::read_to_string(&args.path) {
        Ok(c) => c,
        Err(e) => {
            eprintln!("findr: cannot read {}: {e}", args.path.display());
            return ExitCode::FAILURE;
        }
    };

    let stdout = std::io::stdout();
    let mut out = stdout.lock();

    for line in find_matches(&content, &args.pattern, args.ignore_case) {
        if writeln!(out, "{line}").is_err() {
            // downstream closed the pipe (e.g. `findr x file | head -1`)
            return ExitCode::from(0);
        }
    }

    ExitCode::SUCCESS
}
```

Three conventions separate toys from tools here. **Exit codes are the API**: `0` success, non-zero failure — shell scripts, CI, and other tools key off them, and `ExitCode` makes the contract visible in the signature. **stderr is for humans, stdout is for data**: errors go to `eprintln!`/stderr so that piping (`findr pattern file | wc -l`) stays clean. **SIGPIPE happens**: when the consumer closes early, writing to stdout errors — treating that as success (or a quiet exit) is what makes your tool behave like a good Unix citizen instead of a panic machine. For quick prototypes, `fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>>` gets you partway; explicit `ExitCode` handling is what you graduate to.

## Integration testing: the black box

Unit tests cover the engine; integration tests cover the *product* — the actual binary, invoked exactly as a user would, via **`assert_cmd`** and **`predicates`**:

```toml
[dev-dependencies]
assert_cmd = "2"
predicates = "3"
```

```rust
// tests/cli.rs
use assert_cmd::Command;
use predicates::str::contains;
use std::io::Write;

fn fixture() -> std::path::PathBuf {
    let mut dir = std::env::temp_dir();
    dir.push("findr-test.txt");
    let mut f = std::fs::File::create(&dir).unwrap();
    writeln!(f, "needle in line one").unwrap();
    writeln!(f, "nothing here").unwrap();
    writeln!(f, "second needle").unwrap();
    dir
}

#[test]
fn prints_matching_lines() {
    Command::cargo_bin("findr")
        .unwrap()
        .args(["needle", fixture().to_str().unwrap()])
        .assert()
        .success()
        .stdout(contains("needle in line one"))
        .stdout(contains("second needle"));
}

#[test]
fn missing_file_fails_with_message() {
    Command::cargo_bin("findr")
        .unwrap()
        .args(["x", "/no/such/file"])
        .assert()
        .failure()
        .stderr(contains("cannot read"));
}
```

`Command::cargo_bin` resolves your compiled binary; `.assert()` drives it as a subprocess and checks status, stdout, and stderr. These tests catch what unit tests cannot: argument wiring, exit codes, stream discipline. Together with the unit suite, they are the safety net for every future refactor.

## The release build

Debug builds are for development; `cargo build --release` is what ships — different profile, different numbers:

```toml
[profile.release]
lto = true             # cross-crate inlining: smaller, faster
codegen-units = 1      # better optimization, slower compile
strip = true           # no symbols in the binary
```

Three useful habits. **Benchmark with `hyperfine`** (`hyperfine './target/release/findr pattern file'`) instead of guessing — measure, then tune. **Check the binary size** with `cargo bloat`/`cargo asm` when size matters; `strip` and `lto` often cut it by a third. **Pin your MSRV** (`rust-version = "1.75"` in `Cargo.toml`) so users on older toolchains get a clear error instead of a mystery.

## Distribution: from git to crates.io

The distribution ladder, in ascending order of polish. `cargo install --path .` — local install from source, the developer path. `cargo install findr` — from **crates.io**: run `cargo login`, check `cargo package` (which builds and warns about missing files), then `cargo publish`. The registry is for Rust developers; it is the default expectation of the ecosystem, semver applies, and `cargo doc --open` should have something to say. Finally, **prebuilt binaries** for everyone else: GitHub Releases driven by CI, with `cross` (Docker-based) or `cargo-zigbuild` for targeting Linux/macOS/Windows triples from one machine:

```yaml
# .github/workflows/release.yml (excerpt)
- name: Build
  run: cargo build --release
- name: Release
  uses: softprops/action-gh-release@v2
  with:
    files: target/release/findr
```

A matrix of targets gives macOS, Linux (glibc and musl), and Windows artifacts from one tag push. And when a CLI is the wrong shape — users need windows, buttons, a file picker — the architecture you already built pays off a second time: the `lib.rs` core drops into a Tauri shell unchanged, and the CLI was the first, smallest customer of the same engine.

## Key takeaways

- Split every project into a thin `main.rs` (args, I/O, exit codes) and a testable `lib.rs` core — reuse and testability follow structurally.
- clap's derive turns a documented struct into a full CLI: help, version, validation, and good errors for free.
- Respect the Unix contract: exit codes as API, errors on stderr, data on stdout, and graceful SIGPIPE behavior.
- Test the engine with unit tests, the product with `assert_cmd` integration tests — both, always.
- Ship deliberately: `--release` with `lto`/`strip`, an MSRV in `Cargo.toml`, crates.io for developers, prebuilt binaries via CI for everyone else.

That closes the loop the book opened: nine chapters from a value's single owner to a published binary — the same thread of compile-time guarantees running through all of it. Where you take it next — Tauri cores, services, embedded, a language of your own — the fundamentals travel with you.
